window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-06-2", "source": "2023年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “When faced with differing opinions, we should try to reach agreement through friendly discussion and reasonable argument.” You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words(not including the sentence given).", "sample": "When faced with differing opinions, we should try to reach agreement through friendly discussion and\nreasonable argument. In a world where people come from diverse backgrounds, disagreements are inevitable;\nyet how we handle them reveals our maturity. Rather than shouting others down or stubbornly clinging to our\nown views, seeking common ground through calm reasoning is always the wiser choice.\nThe reason is simple: friendly discussion invites cooperation, while quarrels only deepen the divide. Take the\nfamous debates in scientific communities. When Darwin proposed his theory of evolution, opponents raised\nsharp objections, but progress was made not by insults but by patient argument and shared evidence. Similarly,\nin my own study group, a heated dispute over a project was resolved only after we sat down, listened to one\nanother, and combined our ideas. Mutual respect turned rivals into partners.\nIn conclusion, differing opinions need not lead to conflict. If we approach them with an open mind, sound\nlogic, and genuine respect, we can transform disagreement into understanding and reach a consensus that\nbenefits everyone. This is not only a skill but a mark of a civilized society."}, "translation": {"prompt": "随着中国经济的快速发展和人们生活水平的稳步提高，城市居民对环境和生活品质的要求越来越高。中国地方政府更加注重公共设施的建设和改进，以更好地满足人们的需求。通过兴建新的广场、公园和公共绿地或对原有公共场地重新加以规划改造，许多城市为市民提供了更多休闲和社交的场所。 如今，政府出资购置的健身器械和铺设的健身步道在不少城市随处可见，既明显改善了市民户外活动的条件，又使城市更加美丽。", "sample": "With the rapid development of China's economy and the steady improvement of people's living standards, urban residents are placing increasingly high demands on the environment and the quality of life. To meet these rising demands, Chinese local governments are attaching greater importance to the construction and improvement of public facilities. By building new squares, parks and public green spaces, or redesigning and renovating existing public areas, many cities have provided their citizens with more places for leisure and social activities. Today, government-funded fitness equipment and newly paved fitness trails can be seen everywhere in many cities, which has not only markedly improved the conditions for residents' outdoor activities but also made the cities more beautiful."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2023-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Imagine sitting down to a big dinner: a massive steak, a large portion of fried potatoes, and cake for dessert. After eating so much, you should be too full to eat another bite. But some people experience a powerful urge to keep eating, even after  __26__  in a huge meal, a behavior that makes little sense, as most adults are well-versed in the dangers of obesity, which researchers have shown correlates with  __27__  health issues and is even linked to increased  __28__  risk. But some people still keep eating long after they should stop, a phenomenon Dr. Susan Thompson calls \"insatiable(永不满足的) hunger.\" It is characterized by two main  __29__  : not being satisfied by eating, and having a desire to stay sedentary(久坐不动的).\nThis is at  __30__  with how humans are biologically programmed. When there was a great deal of food available, ancient people would gorge on calories; this massive calorie intake was accompanied by an urge to get active. Humans were also programmed for something called \"compensation,\" which is the brain's  __31__  mechanism for preventing the accumulation of excess weight. With compensation, if you eat one large meal in the morning, you are naturally  __32__  to eat less for the rest of the day.\nBut recent studies show that 70% of American adults have lost the ability to naturally compensate for the calories they consume; worse  __33__  , a significant number of them report  __34__  hunger halfway through an eating session, but, by the end of the meal, they feel the same or higher levels of hunger than when they sat down. Dr. Thompson argues that the main cause of this phenomenon is the modern diet, which is  __35__  of food high in sugar, carbohydrates and calories.", "matchIntro": "The problem with being perfect", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "When psychologist Jessica Pryor lived near an internationally renowned university, she once saw a student walking into a library holding a sleeping bag and a coffee maker. She has heard of graduate students spending 12 to 18 hours at a time in the lab. Their schedules are meant to be literally punishing: If they are scientists-in-training, they won't allow themselves to watch Netflix until their experiments start generating results. \"Relationships become estranged(疏远的)—people stop inviting them to social gatherings or dinner parties, which leads them to spend even more time in the lab,\" Pryor told me."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Along with other therapists, Pryor, who is now with the Family Institute at Northwestern University, is trying to sound the alarm about a tendency among young adults and college students to strive for perfection in their work—sometimes at any cost. Though it is often portrayed as a positive trait, Pryor and others say extreme perfectionism can lead to depression, anxiety, and even suicide."}, {"label": "C", "text": "What's more, perfectionism seems to be on the rise. In a study of thousands of American, Canadian, and British college students published earlier this year, Thomas Curran of the University of Bath and Andrew Hill of York St. John University found that today's college students report higher levels of perfectionism than college students did during the 1990s or early 2000s. They measured three types of perfectionism: self-oriented, or a desire to be perfect; socially prescribed, or a desire to live up to others' expectations; and other-oriented, or holding others to unrealistic standards. From 1989 to 2016, they found, self-oriented perfectionism scores increased by 10 percent, socially prescribed scores rose by 33 percent, and other-oriented perfectionism increased by 16 percent."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A person living with an other-oriented perfectionist might feel criticized by the perfectionist spouse for not doing household chores exactly the \"right\" way. \"One of the most common things couples argue about is the proper way of loading the dishwasher,\" says Amy Bach, a psychologist in Providence, Rhode Island."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Curran describes socially prescribed perfectionism as \"My self-esteem is contingent on what other people think.\" His study didn't examine the causal reasons for its rise, but he assumes that the rise of both standardized testing and social media might play a role. These days, LinkedIn alerts us when our rival gets a new job, and Instagram can let us know how well \"liked\" our lives are compared with a friend's. In an opinion piece earlier this year, Curran and Hill argue that society has also become more dog-eat-dog. \"Over the last 50 years, public interest and civic responsibility have been progressively eroded,\" they write, \"replaced by a focus on self-interest and competition in a supposedly free and open marketplace.\" We strive for perfection, it seems, because we feel we must in order to get ahead. Michael Brustein, a clinical psychologist in Manhattan, says when he first began practicing in 2007, he was surprised by how prevalent perfectionism was among his clients, despite how little his graduate training had focused on the phenomenon. He sees perfectionism in, among others, clients who are entrepreneurs, artists, and tech employees. \"You're in New York because you're ambitious, you have this need to strive,\" he says. \"But then your whole identity gets wrapped into a goal.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Perfectionism can, of course, be a positive force. Think of professional athletes, who train aggressively for ever-higher levels of competition. In well-adjusted perfectionism, someone who doesn't get the gold is able to forget the setback and move on. In maladaptive(不当的) perfectionism, meanwhile, people make an archive of all their failures. They revisit these archives constantly, thinking, as Pryor puts it, \"I need to make myself feel terrible so I don't do this again.\" Then they double down, \"raising the expectation bar even higher, which increases the likelihood of defeat, which makes you self-critical, so you raise the bar higher, work even harder,\" she says. Next comes failure, shame, and pushing yourself even harder toward even higher and more impossible goals. Meeting them becomes an \"all or nothing\" premise. Pryor offered this example: \"Even if I'm an incredible attorney, if I don't make partner in the same pacing as one of my colleagues, clearly that means I'm a failure.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Brustein says his perfectionist clients tend to devalue their accomplishments, so that every time a goal is achieved, the high lasts only a short time, like \"a gas tank with a hole in it.\" If the boss says you did a great job, it's because he doesn't know anything. If the audience likes your work, that's because it's too stupid to know what good art actually is. But, therapists say, there are also different ways perfectionism manifests. Some perfectionists are always pushing themselves forward. But others actually fall behind on work, unable to complete assignments unless they are, well, perfect. Or they might handicap their performance ahead of time. They're the ones partying until 2 a.m. the night before the final, so that when the grade C rolls in, there's a ready excuse."}, {"label": "H", "text": "While educators and parents have successfully convinced students of the need to be high performing and diligent, the experts told me, they haven't adequately prepared them for the inevitability of failure. Instead of praises like \"You're so smart,\" parents and educators should say things like \"You really stuck with it,\" Pryor says, to emphasize the value of perseverance over intrinsic talent. Pryor notes that many of her clients are wary she'll \"turn them into some degenerate couch potato and teach them to be okay with it.\" Instead, she tries to help them think through the parts of their perfectionism they'd like to keep, and to lose the parts that are ruining their lives."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Bach, who sees many students from Brown University, says some of them don't even go out on weekends, let alone weekdays. She tells them, \"Aim high, but get comfortable with good enough.\" When they don't get some award, she encourages them to remember that \"one outcome is not a basis for a broad conclusion about the person's intelligence, qualifications, or potential for the future.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "The treatment for perfectionism might be as simple as having patients keep logs of things they can be proud of, or having them behave imperfectly in small ways, just to see how it feels. \"We might have them hang the towels crooked(不正的) or wear some clothing inside out,\" says Martin Antony, a professor in the department of psychology at Ryerson University in Toronto."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Brustein likes to get his perfectionist clients to create values that are important to them, then try to shift their focus to living according to those values rather than achieving specific goals. It's a play on the \"You really stuck with it\" message for kids. In other words, it isn't about doing a headstand in yoga class; it's about going to yoga class in the first place, because you like to be the kind of person who takes care of herself. But he warns that some people go into therapy expecting too much—an instant transformation of themselves from a pathological(病态的) perfectionist to a (still high-achieving) non-perfectionist. They try to be perfect, in other words, at no longer being perfect."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "How on earth did we come to this? We protect our children obsessively from every harm; we scrutinize every carer, teacher or doctor with whom they come into contact. Yet despite all this, one group, which in no way has their best interests at heart, has almost unconstrained access.\nWe seem to take it for granted that advertisers and marketers are allowed to condition even the youngest children. Before children have even developed a proper sense of their own identity, or learned to handle money, they're encouraged to associate status and self-worth with stuff, and to look to external things such as fame and wealth for validation. We're turning out little consumers rather than young citizens who will value themselves for what they contribute to the society in which they live.\nWe've reached this point so gradually that many of us have never questioned it. It's crept up on us in the 60 years since advertisers started to target the young and found that they could recruit them to a commercial assault on their parents. We've come to know it as \"pester power\" or the ability of children to pressure parents to make certain purchases.\nMany psychologists, child development experts and educators point to research suggesting that this emerging cradle-to-grave consumerism is contributing to growing rates of low self-esteem, depression and other forms of mental illness.\nNot all psychologists agree. There're plenty working hand in glove with a ￡12bn-a-year industry that has turned the manipulation of adult emotions and desires into an art form—often literally. It's also one that's forever developing new ways to persuade our children to desire material possessions, and because of advertisements' viral effect they only need to infect a few to reach the many. Advertising and marketing can serve a useful purpose for children. Marketing may help socialize children as consumers, inform them about products, and help them carve out unique identities as they reach adulthood.\nThen, should we ban all advertising aimed at young children? I say yes.\nOf course there'll be plenty of objections to an outright ban on advertising to the under-11s. There'll be those who argue that would be a breach of freedom of speech and infringe the rights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products.\nMost parents hate what advertising does to their children, but we do have the power to end it and let our children grow up free from many of the pressures of consumerism until they're old enough to make their own decisions. And though advertising is only part of an all-pervasive(无处不在的) marketing culture we need to make a start somewhere. Let's ban all advertising targeting children of primary school age and younger now.", "47": "How on earth did we come to this? We protect our children obsessively from every harm; we scrutinize every carer, teacher or doctor with whom they come into contact. Yet despite all this, one group, which in no way has their best interests at heart, has almost unconstrained access.\nWe seem to take it for granted that advertisers and marketers are allowed to condition even the youngest children. Before children have even developed a proper sense of their own identity, or learned to handle money, they're encouraged to associate status and self-worth with stuff, and to look to external things such as fame and wealth for validation. We're turning out little consumers rather than young citizens who will value themselves for what they contribute to the society in which they live.\nWe've reached this point so gradually that many of us have never questioned it. It's crept up on us in the 60 years since advertisers started to target the young and found that they could recruit them to a commercial assault on their parents. We've come to know it as \"pester power\" or the ability of children to pressure parents to make certain purchases.\nMany psychologists, child development experts and educators point to research suggesting that this emerging cradle-to-grave consumerism is contributing to growing rates of low self-esteem, depression and other forms of mental illness.\nNot all psychologists agree. There're plenty working hand in glove with a ￡12bn-a-year industry that has turned the manipulation of adult emotions and desires into an art form—often literally. It's also one that's forever developing new ways to persuade our children to desire material possessions, and because of advertisements' viral effect they only need to infect a few to reach the many. Advertising and marketing can serve a useful purpose for children. Marketing may help socialize children as consumers, inform them about products, and help them carve out unique identities as they reach adulthood.\nThen, should we ban all advertising aimed at young children? I say yes.\nOf course there'll be plenty of objections to an outright ban on advertising to the under-11s. There'll be those who argue that would be a breach of freedom of speech and infringe the rights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products.\nMost parents hate what advertising does to their children, but we do have the power to end it and let our children grow up free from many of the pressures of consumerism until they're old enough to make their own decisions. And though advertising is only part of an all-pervasive(无处不在的) marketing culture we need to make a start somewhere. Let's ban all advertising targeting children of primary school age and younger now.", "48": "How on earth did we come to this? We protect our children obsessively from every harm; we scrutinize every carer, teacher or doctor with whom they come into contact. Yet despite all this, one group, which in no way has their best interests at heart, has almost unconstrained access.\nWe seem to take it for granted that advertisers and marketers are allowed to condition even the youngest children. Before children have even developed a proper sense of their own identity, or learned to handle money, they're encouraged to associate status and self-worth with stuff, and to look to external things such as fame and wealth for validation. We're turning out little consumers rather than young citizens who will value themselves for what they contribute to the society in which they live.\nWe've reached this point so gradually that many of us have never questioned it. It's crept up on us in the 60 years since advertisers started to target the young and found that they could recruit them to a commercial assault on their parents. We've come to know it as \"pester power\" or the ability of children to pressure parents to make certain purchases.\nMany psychologists, child development experts and educators point to research suggesting that this emerging cradle-to-grave consumerism is contributing to growing rates of low self-esteem, depression and other forms of mental illness.\nNot all psychologists agree. There're plenty working hand in glove with a ￡12bn-a-year industry that has turned the manipulation of adult emotions and desires into an art form—often literally. It's also one that's forever developing new ways to persuade our children to desire material possessions, and because of advertisements' viral effect they only need to infect a few to reach the many. Advertising and marketing can serve a useful purpose for children. Marketing may help socialize children as consumers, inform them about products, and help them carve out unique identities as they reach adulthood.\nThen, should we ban all advertising aimed at young children? I say yes.\nOf course there'll be plenty of objections to an outright ban on advertising to the under-11s. There'll be those who argue that would be a breach of freedom of speech and infringe the rights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products.\nMost parents hate what advertising does to their children, but we do have the power to end it and let our children grow up free from many of the pressures of consumerism until they're old enough to make their own decisions. And though advertising is only part of an all-pervasive(无处不在的) marketing culture we need to make a start somewhere. Let's ban all advertising targeting children of primary school age and younger now.", "49": "How on earth did we come to this? We protect our children obsessively from every harm; we scrutinize every carer, teacher or doctor with whom they come into contact. Yet despite all this, one group, which in no way has their best interests at heart, has almost unconstrained access.\nWe seem to take it for granted that advertisers and marketers are allowed to condition even the youngest children. Before children have even developed a proper sense of their own identity, or learned to handle money, they're encouraged to associate status and self-worth with stuff, and to look to external things such as fame and wealth for validation. We're turning out little consumers rather than young citizens who will value themselves for what they contribute to the society in which they live.\nWe've reached this point so gradually that many of us have never questioned it. It's crept up on us in the 60 years since advertisers started to target the young and found that they could recruit them to a commercial assault on their parents. We've come to know it as \"pester power\" or the ability of children to pressure parents to make certain purchases.\nMany psychologists, child development experts and educators point to research suggesting that this emerging cradle-to-grave consumerism is contributing to growing rates of low self-esteem, depression and other forms of mental illness.\nNot all psychologists agree. There're plenty working hand in glove with a ￡12bn-a-year industry that has turned the manipulation of adult emotions and desires into an art form—often literally. It's also one that's forever developing new ways to persuade our children to desire material possessions, and because of advertisements' viral effect they only need to infect a few to reach the many. Advertising and marketing can serve a useful purpose for children. Marketing may help socialize children as consumers, inform them about products, and help them carve out unique identities as they reach adulthood.\nThen, should we ban all advertising aimed at young children? I say yes.\nOf course there'll be plenty of objections to an outright ban on advertising to the under-11s. There'll be those who argue that would be a breach of freedom of speech and infringe the rights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products.\nMost parents hate what advertising does to their children, but we do have the power to end it and let our children grow up free from many of the pressures of consumerism until they're old enough to make their own decisions. And though advertising is only part of an all-pervasive(无处不在的) marketing culture we need to make a start somewhere. Let's ban all advertising targeting children of primary school age and younger now.", "50": "How on earth did we come to this? We protect our children obsessively from every harm; we scrutinize every carer, teacher or doctor with whom they come into contact. Yet despite all this, one group, which in no way has their best interests at heart, has almost unconstrained access.\nWe seem to take it for granted that advertisers and marketers are allowed to condition even the youngest children. Before children have even developed a proper sense of their own identity, or learned to handle money, they're encouraged to associate status and self-worth with stuff, and to look to external things such as fame and wealth for validation. We're turning out little consumers rather than young citizens who will value themselves for what they contribute to the society in which they live.\nWe've reached this point so gradually that many of us have never questioned it. It's crept up on us in the 60 years since advertisers started to target the young and found that they could recruit them to a commercial assault on their parents. We've come to know it as \"pester power\" or the ability of children to pressure parents to make certain purchases.\nMany psychologists, child development experts and educators point to research suggesting that this emerging cradle-to-grave consumerism is contributing to growing rates of low self-esteem, depression and other forms of mental illness.\nNot all psychologists agree. There're plenty working hand in glove with a ￡12bn-a-year industry that has turned the manipulation of adult emotions and desires into an art form—often literally. It's also one that's forever developing new ways to persuade our children to desire material possessions, and because of advertisements' viral effect they only need to infect a few to reach the many. Advertising and marketing can serve a useful purpose for children. Marketing may help socialize children as consumers, inform them about products, and help them carve out unique identities as they reach adulthood.\nThen, should we ban all advertising aimed at young children? I say yes.\nOf course there'll be plenty of objections to an outright ban on advertising to the under-11s. There'll be those who argue that would be a breach of freedom of speech and infringe the rights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products.\nMost parents hate what advertising does to their children, but we do have the power to end it and let our children grow up free from many of the pressures of consumerism until they're old enough to make their own decisions. And though advertising is only part of an all-pervasive(无处不在的) marketing culture we need to make a start somewhere. Let's ban all advertising targeting children of primary school age and younger now.", "51": "Many oppose workplace surveillance, because of the inherent dehumanizing effect it has and the relentless pressure it brings. But it's on the rise around the world as firms look to become more efficient by squeezing more productivity from their workers. More than half of companies with over $750m in annual revenue used \"non-traditional\" monitoring techniques on staff last year.\nMonitoring employee performance gives firms the ability to assess how their staff are performing and interacting, which can be good for both the firm and employees themselves. A growing number of analytics companies offer this service. They gather \"data exhaust\" left by employees' email and instant messaging apps, and use name badges equipped with radio-frequency identification devices and microphones. These can check how much time you spend talking, your volume and tone of voice, even if you do not dominate conversations. While this may sound intrusive, exponents argue that it can also protect employees against bullying and sexual harassment.\nSome of this data analysis can produce unexpected results. For example, it was found that people who sat at 12-person lunch tables tended to interact, share ideas more and outperform those who regularly sat at four-person tables, a fact that would probably have gone undetected without such data analysis.\nOver the last few years a Stockholm co-working space called Epicenter has gone much further and holds popular \"chipping parties\", where people can have microchips implanted in their hands. They can use the implants to access electronically-controlled doors, or monitor how typing speed correlates with heart rate. Implanted chips may seem extreme, but it is a relatively small step from ID cards and biometrics to such devices.\nAs long as such schemes are voluntary, there will probably be a growing number of convenience-oriented uses so that a substantial number of workers would opt to have a chip inserted. But if implanted chips are used to reduce slack time or rest breaks, that could prove to be detrimental. And if surveillance tools take away autonomy, that's when they prove most unpopular. A lot depends on how such monitoring initiatives are communicated and this could prevent possible revolts being staged.\nIf bosses don't communicate effectively, employees assume the worst. But if they're open about the information they're collecting and what they're doing with it, research suggests 46% of employees are generally okay with it. Although many such monitoring schemes use anonymised data and participation is voluntary, many staffers remain sceptical and fear an erosion of their civil liberties.\nSo workplace surveillance could be empowering for staff and useful for companies looking to become more efficient and profitable. But implemented in the wrong way, it could also become an unpopular tool of oppression that proves counterproductive.", "52": "Many oppose workplace surveillance, because of the inherent dehumanizing effect it has and the relentless pressure it brings. But it's on the rise around the world as firms look to become more efficient by squeezing more productivity from their workers. More than half of companies with over $750m in annual revenue used \"non-traditional\" monitoring techniques on staff last year.\nMonitoring employee performance gives firms the ability to assess how their staff are performing and interacting, which can be good for both the firm and employees themselves. A growing number of analytics companies offer this service. They gather \"data exhaust\" left by employees' email and instant messaging apps, and use name badges equipped with radio-frequency identification devices and microphones. These can check how much time you spend talking, your volume and tone of voice, even if you do not dominate conversations. While this may sound intrusive, exponents argue that it can also protect employees against bullying and sexual harassment.\nSome of this data analysis can produce unexpected results. For example, it was found that people who sat at 12-person lunch tables tended to interact, share ideas more and outperform those who regularly sat at four-person tables, a fact that would probably have gone undetected without such data analysis.\nOver the last few years a Stockholm co-working space called Epicenter has gone much further and holds popular \"chipping parties\", where people can have microchips implanted in their hands. They can use the implants to access electronically-controlled doors, or monitor how typing speed correlates with heart rate. Implanted chips may seem extreme, but it is a relatively small step from ID cards and biometrics to such devices.\nAs long as such schemes are voluntary, there will probably be a growing number of convenience-oriented uses so that a substantial number of workers would opt to have a chip inserted. But if implanted chips are used to reduce slack time or rest breaks, that could prove to be detrimental. And if surveillance tools take away autonomy, that's when they prove most unpopular. A lot depends on how such monitoring initiatives are communicated and this could prevent possible revolts being staged.\nIf bosses don't communicate effectively, employees assume the worst. But if they're open about the information they're collecting and what they're doing with it, research suggests 46% of employees are generally okay with it. Although many such monitoring schemes use anonymised data and participation is voluntary, many staffers remain sceptical and fear an erosion of their civil liberties.\nSo workplace surveillance could be empowering for staff and useful for companies looking to become more efficient and profitable. But implemented in the wrong way, it could also become an unpopular tool of oppression that proves counterproductive.", "53": "Many oppose workplace surveillance, because of the inherent dehumanizing effect it has and the relentless pressure it brings. But it's on the rise around the world as firms look to become more efficient by squeezing more productivity from their workers. More than half of companies with over $750m in annual revenue used \"non-traditional\" monitoring techniques on staff last year.\nMonitoring employee performance gives firms the ability to assess how their staff are performing and interacting, which can be good for both the firm and employees themselves. A growing number of analytics companies offer this service. They gather \"data exhaust\" left by employees' email and instant messaging apps, and use name badges equipped with radio-frequency identification devices and microphones. These can check how much time you spend talking, your volume and tone of voice, even if you do not dominate conversations. While this may sound intrusive, exponents argue that it can also protect employees against bullying and sexual harassment.\nSome of this data analysis can produce unexpected results. For example, it was found that people who sat at 12-person lunch tables tended to interact, share ideas more and outperform those who regularly sat at four-person tables, a fact that would probably have gone undetected without such data analysis.\nOver the last few years a Stockholm co-working space called Epicenter has gone much further and holds popular \"chipping parties\", where people can have microchips implanted in their hands. They can use the implants to access electronically-controlled doors, or monitor how typing speed correlates with heart rate. Implanted chips may seem extreme, but it is a relatively small step from ID cards and biometrics to such devices.\nAs long as such schemes are voluntary, there will probably be a growing number of convenience-oriented uses so that a substantial number of workers would opt to have a chip inserted. But if implanted chips are used to reduce slack time or rest breaks, that could prove to be detrimental. And if surveillance tools take away autonomy, that's when they prove most unpopular. A lot depends on how such monitoring initiatives are communicated and this could prevent possible revolts being staged.\nIf bosses don't communicate effectively, employees assume the worst. But if they're open about the information they're collecting and what they're doing with it, research suggests 46% of employees are generally okay with it. Although many such monitoring schemes use anonymised data and participation is voluntary, many staffers remain sceptical and fear an erosion of their civil liberties.\nSo workplace surveillance could be empowering for staff and useful for companies looking to become more efficient and profitable. But implemented in the wrong way, it could also become an unpopular tool of oppression that proves counterproductive.", "54": "Many oppose workplace surveillance, because of the inherent dehumanizing effect it has and the relentless pressure it brings. But it's on the rise around the world as firms look to become more efficient by squeezing more productivity from their workers. More than half of companies with over $750m in annual revenue used \"non-traditional\" monitoring techniques on staff last year.\nMonitoring employee performance gives firms the ability to assess how their staff are performing and interacting, which can be good for both the firm and employees themselves. A growing number of analytics companies offer this service. They gather \"data exhaust\" left by employees' email and instant messaging apps, and use name badges equipped with radio-frequency identification devices and microphones. These can check how much time you spend talking, your volume and tone of voice, even if you do not dominate conversations. While this may sound intrusive, exponents argue that it can also protect employees against bullying and sexual harassment.\nSome of this data analysis can produce unexpected results. For example, it was found that people who sat at 12-person lunch tables tended to interact, share ideas more and outperform those who regularly sat at four-person tables, a fact that would probably have gone undetected without such data analysis.\nOver the last few years a Stockholm co-working space called Epicenter has gone much further and holds popular \"chipping parties\", where people can have microchips implanted in their hands. They can use the implants to access electronically-controlled doors, or monitor how typing speed correlates with heart rate. Implanted chips may seem extreme, but it is a relatively small step from ID cards and biometrics to such devices.\nAs long as such schemes are voluntary, there will probably be a growing number of convenience-oriented uses so that a substantial number of workers would opt to have a chip inserted. But if implanted chips are used to reduce slack time or rest breaks, that could prove to be detrimental. And if surveillance tools take away autonomy, that's when they prove most unpopular. A lot depends on how such monitoring initiatives are communicated and this could prevent possible revolts being staged.\nIf bosses don't communicate effectively, employees assume the worst. But if they're open about the information they're collecting and what they're doing with it, research suggests 46% of employees are generally okay with it. Although many such monitoring schemes use anonymised data and participation is voluntary, many staffers remain sceptical and fear an erosion of their civil liberties.\nSo workplace surveillance could be empowering for staff and useful for companies looking to become more efficient and profitable. But implemented in the wrong way, it could also become an unpopular tool of oppression that proves counterproductive.", "55": "Many oppose workplace surveillance, because of the inherent dehumanizing effect it has and the relentless pressure it brings. But it's on the rise around the world as firms look to become more efficient by squeezing more productivity from their workers. More than half of companies with over $750m in annual revenue used \"non-traditional\" monitoring techniques on staff last year.\nMonitoring employee performance gives firms the ability to assess how their staff are performing and interacting, which can be good for both the firm and employees themselves. A growing number of analytics companies offer this service. They gather \"data exhaust\" left by employees' email and instant messaging apps, and use name badges equipped with radio-frequency identification devices and microphones. These can check how much time you spend talking, your volume and tone of voice, even if you do not dominate conversations. While this may sound intrusive, exponents argue that it can also protect employees against bullying and sexual harassment.\nSome of this data analysis can produce unexpected results. For example, it was found that people who sat at 12-person lunch tables tended to interact, share ideas more and outperform those who regularly sat at four-person tables, a fact that would probably have gone undetected without such data analysis.\nOver the last few years a Stockholm co-working space called Epicenter has gone much further and holds popular \"chipping parties\", where people can have microchips implanted in their hands. They can use the implants to access electronically-controlled doors, or monitor how typing speed correlates with heart rate. Implanted chips may seem extreme, but it is a relatively small step from ID cards and biometrics to such devices.\nAs long as such schemes are voluntary, there will probably be a growing number of convenience-oriented uses so that a substantial number of workers would opt to have a chip inserted. But if implanted chips are used to reduce slack time or rest breaks, that could prove to be detrimental. And if surveillance tools take away autonomy, that's when they prove most unpopular. A lot depends on how such monitoring initiatives are communicated and this could prevent possible revolts being staged.\nIf bosses don't communicate effectively, employees assume the worst. But if they're open about the information they're collecting and what they're doing with it, research suggests 46% of employees are generally okay with it. Although many such monitoring schemes use anonymised data and participation is voluntary, many staffers remain sceptical and fear an erosion of their civil liberties.\nSo workplace surveillance could be empowering for staff and useful for companies looking to become more efficient and profitable. But implemented in the wrong way, it could also become an unpopular tool of oppression that proves counterproductive."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "Why does the woman wish to study architecture?", "options": ["A) She is attracted to the beauty of modern buildings.", "B) She is preoccupied with her dream to be an architect.", "C) She is influenced by her father who teaches architecture.", "D) She is drawn to its integration of design and engineering."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is drawn to its integration of design and engineering.\n【定位】In particular, I'm attracted to the marriage of design and engineering.\n尤其是，我被设计与工程的结合所吸引。\n【信号】被问者直接应答动机。教授先问 Why did you choose it?，她的回答里用 I'm attracted to\n直接给出动机，且以 In particular 加重强调——\"问因 + 被问者亲口应答 + 加重语气\"三重\n信号锁定此句。\n【替换】the marriage of design and engineering ↔ its integration of design and engineering\nmarriage=integration同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 I'm attracted to，A 项 She is attracted to the beauty of modern buildings.\n借用了 attracted 这个原词，但她被吸引的是\"设计与工程的结合\"，不是\"现代建筑的\n美\"——美这一点录音从未说，属听到 attracted 就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：I'm attracted to the marriage of design and engineering\nB) 原词陷阱\npreoccupied 一词录音里出现在教授口中 you were preoccupied with all the lofty\nmathematics，指她曾为数学发愁，根本不是\"痴迷当建筑师的梦想\"。B 项 She is\npreoccupied with her dream to be an architect. 盗了 preoccupied 又改了对象，与\n问\"动机\"无关。\n借用原文：you were preoccupied with all the lofty mathematics\nC) 未提及\nC 项 She is influenced by her father who teaches architecture. 牵出一个\"教建筑的父\n亲\"，但整段录音从未提到她父亲，更没说受其影响——是预读时就警惕的\"凭空多出一个人\n物\"的诱选。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "How does the woman say she will overcome the difficulty in learning mathematics?", "options": ["A) Through hard work.", "B) With the professor's help.", "C) By studying the subject online.", "D) By taking prerequisite courses."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Through hard work.\n【定位】Math might be the toughest subject I encounter in the curriculum, but I'll simply put in the\nwork required.\n数学可能是我在课程里遇到的最难的科目，但我会直接付出所需的努力。\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真应对。她先承认 Math might be the toughest subject，紧接 but I'll\nsimply put in the work required——转折词 but 之后才是她真正给出的克服办法，前半\n只是铺垫难处。\"but 后是落点\"是听力定位的硬信号。\n【替换】put in the work required ↔ Through hard work\nput in work=hard work同义概括\n【排除】B) 未提及\n对话里确有 professor 这个人，B 项 With the professor's help. 借\"现场有教授\"诱你选，\n但她讲克服数学的办法时只字未提靠教授，靠的是自己出力——属\"同场有这人就误以为靠\n他\"的陷阱。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 I was doing some online research 确实出现 online，C 项 By studying the\nsubject online. 盗了 online。但她上网只是\"做了点调研\"判断自己应付得来，并非\"靠网上\n学数学\"来克服难点——原词在、用途被偷换。\n借用原文：I was doing some online research\nD) 未提及\nD 项 By taking prerequisite courses. 说靠\"修先修课\"克服，但录音从未提到 prerequisite\n或先修课程这回事——是凭空安上的办法。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the man say about architects' contribution to a city?", "options": ["A) It is groundbreaking.", "B) It is long-lasting.", "C) It is immaterial.", "D) It is immortal."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is long-lasting.\n【定位】And the legacies you build will last much longer than any of us mortals.\n而且你所建造的那些遗产，会比我们这些凡人存续得久得多。\n【信号】男士对城市贡献的直接断言。男士谈到 the contribution you make to a city 后，紧接断\n言 the legacies you build will last much longer——这句直接定性\"建筑师留下的东西能\n存续很久\"，是他对该话题的正面陈述句，定位于此。\n【替换】will last much longer than any of us mortals ↔ It is long-lasting\nlast much longer=long-lasting同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 It is groundbreaking.\"具开创性\"在录音里毫无对应——男士谈的是贡献能\"长久存\n续\"，不是\"开天辟地\"，属凭空贴标签。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音里 Due to its material nature 出现 material（实体的），C 项 It is immaterial. 恰好\n取了它的反义 immaterial（非物质/无关紧要）。建筑明明是\"实体\"，选项却说\"无形\"，\n是\"盗原词又反极性\"的诱选。\n借用原文：Due to its material nature\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音 longer than any of us mortals 用 mortals（凡人）作对比，D 项 It is immortal. 盗\n这个词根改成 immortal（不朽）。原文只说贡献\"比凡人活得久\"，是相对的\"更久\"，并\n非\"永不灭\"——把相对的久夸成绝对不朽，程度过头。\n借用原文：will last much longer than any of us mortals", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What subject does the man suggest the woman start with?", "options": ["A) Economics.", "B) Philosophy.", "C) Computer science.", "D) Western art."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Computer science.\n【定位】Nevertheless, I would suggest you start with computer science because there will be many\nintricate visuals you will have to produce as part of your projects.\n不过，我建议你从计算机科学开始，因为你的项目里会有很多复杂的图像需要你来制作。\n【信号】Nevertheless 转折 + suggest start with。前句先泛举 philosophy and economics 等\n领域，再用 Nevertheless 一转，紧跟 I would suggest you start with computer science\n——转折后的\"建议 + start with\"双信号，直指真正要先学的那门。\n【替换】I would suggest you start with computer science ↔ Computer science\n原句点名即选项学科\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nphilosophy and economics 里 economics 被念到，但那是男士泛举\"大学会鼓励你学的各\n类领域\"，A 项 Economics. 盗这个原词冒充答案；题问的是 start with（先学），经济学并\n非他建议起步的那门。\n借用原文：fields as wide-ranging as philosophy and economics\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项 Philosophy. 同样来自 philosophy and economics 的泛举，philosophy 被念到却不\n是\"建议先学\"的那门——与 A 一样是\"听到学科名就想划\"的原词诱选。\n借用原文：fields as wide-ranging as philosophy and economics\nD) 答非所问\n录音末尾 the history of Western art 确出现\"西方艺术\"，D 项 Western art. 盗了它。但那\n是男士另起一句\"还建议你尽量多学美术\"，与本题问的 start with（最先入手）不是同一件\n事——位置和性质都对不上。\n借用原文：another thing that I would recommend you learn as much as you can is fine arts, in\nparticular, the history of Western art", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn from the conversation about Mr. Sanchez?", "options": ["A) He has occasionally been harassed by his fans.", "B) He has been guarded by a discreet assistant.", "C) He is well known to the public.", "D) He is a famous football coach."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He is well known to the public.\n【定位】As we all know, being such a famous football player at Real Madrid, Mr. Sanchez has a\nvery high public profile. This means he cannot leave the house without being recognized\nand harassed.\n众所周知，作为皇马一名如此有名的足球运动员，桑切斯先生有着很高的公众知名度。这意\n味着他一出门就会被认出、被骚扰。\n【信号】直接的身份介绍句。女士用 being such a famous football player at Real Madrid 一句话\n给桑切斯定了身份，再用 has a very high public profile 说明其名气之大——这种\"客观介\n绍人物\"的陈述句就是身份细节题的答案句。\n【替换】has a very high public profile ↔ well known to the public\n「高公众知名度」=「被公众熟知」\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHe has occasionally been harassed by his fans. 借了录音 recognized and harassed 里\n的 harassed 一词，但录音说的是\"一出门就\"被骚扰、且并未限定\"被粉丝\"，更没说\"偶\n尔\"。题问的是\"我们了解到什么\"，最核心是他名气大，A把附带后果当主答案、还加了\noccasionally,fans 这些原文没有的限定。\n借用原文：recognized and harassed\nB) 张冠李戴\nHe has been guarded by a discreet assistant. 把开头 we are looking for someone\nwho is very discreet 里的 discreet 拿来，安成\"他被一位谨慎的助理护卫\"。但 discreet 是\n对应聘者（男方）的要求，不是说桑切斯有这样一个助理——人物对应被搬错，且题问的是\n桑切斯本人，不是他的助理。\n借用原文：someone who is very discreet\nD) 偷换概念\nHe is a famous football coach. 借用了录音 famous football player 里的 famous\nfootball，却把 player（球员）偷换成 coach（教练）。一字之差、身份全错，这正是预读\n时圈出\"coach 是差异点\"要防的偷换。\n借用原文：famous football player", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What will the man do if he gets the job?", "options": ["A) Serve as a personal assistant.", "B) Play a key role in Real Madrid.", "C) Run common daily chores for the woman.", "D) Help promote Mr. Sanchez's public profile."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Serve as a personal assistant.\n【定位】As his personal assistant, you would be required to run common everyday chores for Mr.\nSanchez, things he cannot do himself due to his fame, like going to the supermarket or\npost office.\n作为他的私人助理，你将被要求为桑切斯先生跑日常琐事，那些他因名气太大而无法自己做\n的事，比如去超市或邮局。\n【信号】招聘者直接交代岗位。女方用 As his personal assistant 一句明确点出岗位名，这种由招聘\n方直接交代\"你将担任什么\"的句子，就是\"他将做什么\"的答案句。\n【替换】As his personal assistant ↔ Serve as a personal assistant.\n「作为私人助理」=「担任私人助理」\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nPlay a key role in Real Madrid. 借了第7、8段反复出现的 Real Madrid，把\"跑腿助理\"夸\n成\"在皇马担任关键角色\"。录音清楚说他要做的是 run common everyday chores（跑日\n常琐事），与\"key role\"完全相反，是过度拔高。\n借用原文：run common everyday chores for Mr. Sanchez\nC) 张冠李戴\nRun common daily chores for the woman. 几乎照搬录音 run common everyday\nchores，只把\"for Mr. Sanchez\"换成\"for the woman\"。跑腿是为桑切斯、不是为这位招聘\n的女士，服务对象被掉包——正是预读时圈 for the woman 要防的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：run common everyday chores for Mr. Sanchez\nD) 与原文相反\nHelp promote Mr. Sanchez's public profile. 拿了 high public profile 里的 public\nprofile，编成\"帮桑切斯提升公众形象\"。但录音说的是因他名气太大、助理替他做他没法做\n的事（things he cannot do himself due to his fame），是\"避开曝光\"，绝非\"提升曝\n光\"，方向相反。\n借用原文：has a very high public profile", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man say about himself?", "options": ["A) He is honest and always tells the truth.", "B) He once worked part-time in university.", "C) He cares little about his working hours.", "D) He has little previous work experience."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He has little previous work experience.\n【定位】This is my first job after graduating from university. So frankly, I don't have much prior\nworking experience.\n这是我大学毕业后的第一份工作。所以坦白说，我没有多少之前的工作经验。\n【信号】第一人称的自我坦白。男方用 This is my first job after graduating from university 加 I\ndon't have much prior working experience 直陈自身处境，这种\"I/my\"领起的自述句即\n本题答案落点。\n【替换】I don't have much prior working experience ↔ He has little previous work experience.\n「没多少经验」=「几乎没经验」\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nHe is honest and always tells the truth. 大概由录音里 frankly（坦白说）联想而来，但\nfrankly 只是说话的语气词，男方并未谈自己\"诚实、总说真话\"。把一个口头副词夸成性格\n优点，属无中生有的推断。\n借用原文：So frankly, I don't have much prior working experience\nB) 未提及\nHe once worked part-time in university. 用了 university 这个录音确实出现的词（after\ngraduating from university），但男方只说\"大学毕业\"，从未提\"在校期间打过工\"。借眼\n熟的 university 编出一段没说过的经历，属未提及。\n借用原文：after graduating from university\nC) 未提及\nHe cares little about his working hours. 听起来像呼应他\"愿从底层做起\"（keen to start\nfrom the bottom）的谦逊，但他谈的是不计职位与薪水，从没说\"不在乎工作时长\n（working hours）\"。working hours 是录音里完全没出现的话题。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why does the man think he is a very competitive candidate?", "options": ["A) He has a strong ability to connect with people.", "B) He has a high proficiency in several languages.", "C) He has a sound knowledge of sports consultancy.", "D) He has a natural capacity to cooperate with others."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He has a high proficiency in several languages.\n【定位】And I think my language skills could prove very useful down the road.\n而且我觉得我的语言能力日后会很有用。\n【信号】他自陈的优势落点。在列举理由的段落里，男方以 I think my language skills could prove\nvery useful 把\"语言能力\"点为自己的可用之长——这种\"I think my … useful\"的自夸句\n就是优势题的答案句。第10段又用 fully fluent in English and Spanish 坐实他确有多语\n能力。\n【替换】my language skills could prove very useful ↔ He has a high proficiency in several languages.\n「语言能力有用」=「精通几门语言」\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHe has a strong ability to connect with people. 描述的人际沟通能力，录音通篇没有提\n及。他列的是爱足球、觉得公司酷、语言能力，没有任何一句谈\"与人连接\"，属凭\"助理该会\n沟通\"的常识脑补。\nC) 张冠李戴\nHe has a sound knowledge of sports consultancy. 盗用了录音 what this company\ndoes in sports consultancy 里的 sports consultancy。但这是他在夸\"公司做的体育咨询\n很酷\"，说的是公司的业务，不是他本人懂体育咨询——把公司的事安成了他的能力。\n借用原文：what this company does in sports consultancy is very cool\nD) 未提及\nHe has a natural capacity to cooperate with others. 和A一样属人际类能力，与他亲口列\n的\"语言能力\"无关，录音没说他\"善于与人协作\"。它和A刻意做得相像（connect with\npeople/cooperate with others），用来分散对正确项B的注意。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "Why do extreme sports appeal to many kids?", "options": ["A) They bring more benefits to young people.", "B) They require less supervision and training.", "C) They are more suitable to young people.", "D) They have fewer rules and pressures."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have fewer rules and pressures.\n【定位】They don't have many of the rules and pressures of traditional team sports, but they still have\nall the benefits of outdoor physical activity.\n它们没有传统团队运动那么多的规则和压力，但仍然具备户外体育活动的全部益处。\n【信号】对照句\"没有……但仍有……\"。这句用 They don't have many of the rules and pressures\nof traditional team sports 与后面 but they still have all the benefits 做对照，前半把\"规\n则和压力少\"点了出来——这正是这类运动区别于传统运动、吸引孩子的卖点，是承载答案\n的句子。\n【替换】don't have many of the rules and pressures ↔ have fewer rules and pressures\ndon't have many = fewer\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了录音同句里的原词 benefits，但录音说的是\"仍具备户外活动的全部益处\"，并非\"带\n来更多益处\"；more 是选项硬加的，题目问的是\"吸引力来自哪\"，benefits 只是顺带提及，\n不是吸引点。\n借用原文：they still have all the benefits of outdoor physical activity\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 的 less supervision and training 借用了第二段的 When properly supervised 制造熟\n耳感，但录音从没说这类运动\"需要的监管和训练更少\"；相反第二段强调\"需要妥善监管\"，\n与 B 相反。\n借用原文：When properly supervised, extreme sports can be a part of a healthy, balanced life.\nC) 未提及\nC 说这类运动\"更适合\"年轻人，录音只说它们对孩子\"有吸引力（attractive/appeal）\"，从\n未做\"更适合/更不适合\"的比较判断，属于原文未提及的拔高。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the speaker say about risk-free activities?", "options": ["A) They prevent kids from enjoying adventure sports.", "B) They help kids guard against any possible injuries.", "C) They rob kids of the chance to cultivate their courage.", "D) They deprive kids of the opportunity to develop team spirit."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They rob kids of the chance to cultivate their courage.\n【定位】Risk-free activities deprive kids of the opportunity to test themselves and overcome\ntheir fears.\n无风险的活动剥夺了孩子自我考验、克服恐惧的机会。\n【信号】对象词 risk-free 直接现身。录音里 Risk-free activities deprive kids of the opportunity\nto test themselves and overcome their fears 正面点出题干对象 risk-free activities，并\n紧跟一个负面动词 deprive，这一句就是对该对象的直接评价，是答案句。\n【替换】deprive kids of the opportunity to test themselves and overcome their fears ↔\nrob kids of the chance to cultivate their courage\ndeprive=rob、克服恐惧=培养勇气\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 prevent kids from enjoying adventure sports 借了录音常出现的 adventure\nsports，但录音说\"剥夺机会\"的是 risk-free activities 对\"自我考验\"，并非\"阻止孩子享受冒\n险运动\"，把对象和内容都换了。\n借用原文：Adventure sports have many of the same risks and dangers\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说无风险活动\"帮孩子防受伤\"，听上去像好事，但录音对 risk-free activities 的态度是负\n面的（deprive 剥夺机会），B 把贬义说成了褒义，方向相反；injuries 一词来自录音第二\n段对 devastating injuries 的描述。\n借用原文：extreme sports can often lead to devastating injuries\nD) 偷换概念\nD 把录音\"克服恐惧\"偷换成 develop team spirit（团队精神）。录音里\"团队\"概念只与\ntraditional team sports 沾边，从没说无风险活动剥夺了\"团队精神\"，是把尾词悄悄换掉。\n借用原文：traditional team sports", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What should parents do if they decide adventure sports are a bit too much for their kids?", "options": ["A) Help them take up these sports when they are more mature.", "B) Let them participate in some less risky outdoor activities.", "C) Ask them to try some forms of indoor sports.", "D) Introduce them to these sports step by step."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Let them participate in some less risky outdoor activities.\n【定位】But if you do decide that adventure sports are a bit too much, do try to introduce other forms\nof outdoor adventures into their lives.\n但如果你确实觉得冒险运动有点过头，那就一定要试着把其他形式的户外探险引入他们的\n生活。\n【信号】建议句\"do try to…\"加重语气。这句用 do try to introduce other forms of outdoor\nadventures，do 加在动词前是强调语气的建议，且与题干条件 adventure sports are a\nbit too much 逐字对上，是承载答案的建议句。\n【替换】introduce other forms of outdoor adventures ↔ participate in some less risky outdoor activities\n其他户外探险 = 风险更低的户外活动\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说\"等孩子更成熟再让他们玩这些运动\"，录音从没建议\"等长大再玩\"，only 提到引入别的\n户外活动；mature 一词录音也未出现，属凭空添加的方案。\nC) 与原文相反\nC 让孩子\"试室内运动\"，但录音建议的是 outdoor adventures（户外探险），并举露营、\n观星等户外例子，方向与\"室内\"相反。\n借用原文：introduce other forms of outdoor adventures into their lives\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的\"step by step / 循序渐进地把这些运动介绍给孩子\"盗用了录音原词 introduce，但录\n音的 introduce 接的是 other forms of outdoor adventures（别的户外活动），不是\"循\n序渐进地引入冒险运动本身\"；step by step 是选项硬加，且与\"觉得太过头就别玩\"的前提\n矛盾。\n借用原文：do try to introduce other forms of outdoor adventures into their lives", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why do new tech products soon become waste?", "options": ["A) Consumers often have a craving for the latest model.", "B) Such products tend to comprise parts that are irreplaceable.", "C) Tech firms intentionally design products to have short lifespans.", "D) Manufacturers use effective strategies to promote fancier products."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Tech firms intentionally design products to have short lifespans.\n【定位】In fact, technology firms often purposely design devices that will not last long and cannot be\nrepaired so that consumers will have to spend their money on a new one.\n事实上，科技公司常常故意把设备设计成用不长久、又无法维修，好让消费者不得不掏钱买\n新的。\n【信号】强调词 In fact + 因果连词 so that。In fact 把语气加重、引出真意，紧接的 so that\nconsumers will have to spend their money on a new one 是明确因果，正是 Why 题要\n锁定的那一句。\n【替换】purposely design devices that will not last long ↔\nintentionally design products to have short lifespans\npurposely=intentionally，短命换说法\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 spend their money on a new one 里有 \"买新\" 的影子，但那是厂商设计造\n成的结果，不是说消费者主动 \"渴望新款\"；A 盗用 \"new\" 的语感把原因偷换到消费者欲望\n上。\n借用原文：consumers will have to spend their money on a new one\nB) 答非所问\n录音说 cannot be repaired，B 据此说零件 irreplaceable，但题问的是 \"为何变废\" 的根因\n——厂商故意，而非单纯零件不可换；B 抓了枝节当主因。\n借用原文：cannot be repaired\nD) 偷换概念\n录音批评厂商 purposely design devices that will not last long，D 却说厂商用有效策略\n推广更高端产品，语气由贬转中、且 \"fancier products\" 录音根本没说，是改了立场的无中\n生有。\n借用原文：purposely design devices that will not last long and cannot be repaired", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What did the French government require tech companies to do to help consumers make better choices?", "options": ["A) Indicate the competitiveness of their products.", "B) List a repairability score of their products.", "C) Specify the major parts of their products.", "D) Detail the lifecycle of their products."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） List a repairability score of their products.\n【定位】This year, the French government began requiring tech manufacturers to list a\nrepairability score.\n今年，法国政府开始要求科技制造商列出一个可维修性评分。\n【信号】题干主体 French government 在原文复现，紧跟其后动作即答案。录音 the French\ngovernment began requiring tech manufacturers to list a repairability score 与题干主\n体逐字对上，requiring 后的动作就是要求内容，是顺序定位的标准信号。\n【替换】list a repairability score ↔ List a repairability score of their products\n近乎原句对应，list 与评分逐词吻合\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇没说要标 \"竞争力\"，A 的 competitiveness of their products 是凭 \"产品\" 话题臆\n造出的方向，与政府要求无关。\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音第一段说设备 cannot be repaired 时隐含零件问题，C 借 major parts 之名说政府要\n求 \"列出主要零件\"，但政府要求的是评分不是列零件，把别处的词安到这道题上。\n借用原文：cannot be repaired\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音后句提到 its life can be extended，D 的 lifecycle of their products 借这点说要求\n\"详述生命周期\"，但那是评分带来的好处，不是政府要求标注的内容，方向被带偏。\n借用原文：its life can be extended", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What should tech companies do to help ensure environmental sustainability?", "options": ["A) Take the initiative to reduce electronic waste.", "B) Take due caution in upgrading their products.", "C) Invest in constructing more recycling facilities.", "D) Substitute all toxic substances with non-toxic ones."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Take the initiative to reduce electronic waste.\n【定位】We need tech companies to take the lead to solve this problem.\n我们需要科技公司带头来解决这个问题。\n【信号】主张句信号 We need…to take the lead。We need tech companies to take the lead\nto solve this problem 用 We need…to 直陈主张，take the lead 是 \"应做\" 动作的核心，\n正是 should 题要锁的句。\n【替换】take the lead to solve this problem ↔ Take the initiative to reduce electronic waste\ntake the lead=take the initiative；this problem=electronic waste\n【排除】B) 未提及\nTake due caution in upgrading their products 谈升级要谨慎，录音只在第一段批评厂商\n频出新品，从未要求厂商 \"升级时谨慎\"，是顺着话题臆造的对策。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 Batteries explode in recycling facilities 是在讲危害，C 借 recycling facilities 这个\n原词说应 \"投资建更多回收设施\"，但录音没把建设施当对策，把危害描述里的词挪来当答\n案。\n借用原文：Batteries explode in recycling facilities\nD) 过度推断\n录音 Toxic substances like mercury leak into soil 只在说毒物危害，D 借 toxic\nsubstances 拔高成 \"用无毒物替换全部毒物\"，substitute all 录音根本没主张，绝对词 all\n更是过满。\n借用原文：Toxic substances like mercury leak into soil and groundwater", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the speaker think of the problem of waste?", "options": ["A) It can be solved.", "B) It is certain to worsen.", "C) It will be fixed by tech companies.", "D) It is unavoidable in the long run."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It can be solved.\n【定位】But we cannot leave things broken: A problem of our creation is a problem that can be fixed.\n但我们不能让问题就这么烂着：我们自己造成的问题，是能够被解决的。\n【信号】转折 But + 末句定调 can be fixed。But we cannot leave things broken 的 But 是态度\n反转信号，前句 damage is a certainty 是铺垫，转折后的 a problem that can be fixed\n才是说话人真正的乐观结论。\n【替换】a problem that can be fixed ↔ It can be solved.\ncan be fixed=can be solved，主被动同形\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音 damage is a certainty 只说损害必定存在，B 借 certainty 这个词头偷换成 certain\nto worsen（必然变糟），把 \"必定有损害\" 曲解为 \"必定恶化\"，与说话人乐观结论相反。\n借用原文：damage is a certainty\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音说问题 can be fixed 是 \"我们\" 共同的事，C 却说 fixed by tech companies，把解决\n主体单独安到科技公司头上；录音前文还说很多厂商 lobby against repair，正说明不能指\n望它们独力解决。\n借用原文：many lobby against repair legislation\nD) 与原文相反\nunavoidable in the long run 借的是 damage is a certainty 的悲观语气，但说话人紧接\nBut 就反转为问题能解决，D 取了被否定掉的那一半，与最终乐观态度相反。\n借用原文：damage is a certainty", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does most cyberloafing research focus on?", "options": ["A) How internet monitoring can be implemented.", "B) How to encourage productive internet surfing.", "C) How cyberloafing affects overall productivity.", "D) How to prevent employees from cyberloafing."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） How to prevent employees from cyberloafing.\n【定位】The majority of cyberloafing research focuses on ways to prevent employees from engaging\nin this behavior through interventions such as internet monitoring and computer use policies.\n大多数关于摸鱼上网的研究关注的是如何通过网络监控、电脑使用规定等干预手段来防止员\n工从事这种行为。\n【信号】原话直述 research focuses on。录音直接说 The majority of cyberloafing research\nfocuses on ways to prevent employees from engaging in this behavior，与题干\nresearch focus on 几乎同词，是直述型答案句，听到 research focuses on 立刻锁定其后\n的宾语。\n【替换】ways to prevent employees from engaging in this behavior ↔\nHow to prevent employees from cyberloafing\nthis behavior 即 cyberloafing，prevent 原词对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念了 internet monitoring，但它是研究焦点（防止摸鱼）下面举的干预手段之\n一，不是研究本身关注的对象；A 把举例升格成焦点，且原文没说研究关注它如何被\nimplemented。\n借用原文：internet monitoring and computer use policies\nB) 与原文相反\n原文研究关注的是防止摸鱼，B 却说成鼓励高效上网（encourage productive internet\nsurfing），方向相反，录音从未说大多数研究关注怎么鼓励上网。\n借用原文：prevent employees from engaging in this behavior\nC) 张冠李戴\ncyberloafing 对效率的影响是讲者自己团队后续探讨的内容，而本题问的是大多数已有研究\n关注什么——答案句明说那聚焦于 prevent；C 张冠李戴，把后文结论安到大多数研究头\n上。\n借用原文：is cyberloafing actually effective at reducing employee stress levels?", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What was the hypothesis of the speaker's research team?", "options": ["A) Cyberloafing is a sign of workers' laziness.", "B) Cyberloafing may relieve employees of stress.", "C) Employee engagement is closely related to job satisfaction.", "D) Overuse of social media may lead to decline in productivity."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Cyberloafing may relieve employees of stress.\n【定位】Our hypothesis was that cyberloafing may serve as a mini-break, giving employees an\nopportunity to recover from stressful work situations.\n我们的假设是：摸鱼上网或许能充当一次短暂的休息，给员工一个从高压工作处境中恢复过\n来的机会。\n【信号】标志词 Our hypothesis was that 引出答案。录音用 Our hypothesis was that\ncyberloafing may serve as a mini-break 直接点出假设，hypothesis 与题干同词，是标\n志词引导的答案句，听到 hypothesis was that 后面那句就是答案。\n【替换】recover from stressful work situations ↔ relieve employees of stress\n从压力中恢复＝缓解压力，同义改写\nserve as a mini-break ↔ may relieve employees of stress\n充当短歇＝起到减压作用，功能等值\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音确实念了 a sign that a worker is lazy，但前面有 may not be，是讲者要否定的旧观\n念，不是其团队的假设；A 取了被否定的那一极，正话反取。\n借用原文：such behaviors may not be a sign that a worker is lazy\nC) 张冠李戴\nhigher levels of employee engagement and job satisfaction 是录音引用的他人研究背\n景，且原文是说社媒使用与二者相关，并未说 engagement 与 job satisfaction 互相关\n联；C 既错置主体又乱搭关系。\n借用原文：Social media use at work has been linked to higher levels of employee engagement and\njob satisfaction\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音多处提到 productivity，但本团队的假设是摸鱼减压，而非过度用社媒会降低效率；D\n取了一个原文别处的话题词拼成假设，答非所问。\n借用原文：cost employers up to $85 billion a year in lost productivity", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "On what point do the results of the speaker's study agree with other recent research?", "options": ["A) Taking mini-breaks means better job performance.", "B) Cyberloafing generally does more harm than good.", "C) Worker turnover is linked to the time allowed for cyberloafing.", "D) Employees who indulge in internet surfing are most likely to quit."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Taking mini-breaks means better job performance.\n【定位】This fits with other recent research that suggests taking short breaks is associated with higher\nlevels of daily job performance.\n这与其他近期研究相吻合——那些研究表明，进行短暂的休息与更高水平的日常工作表现相\n关联。\n【信号】fits with other recent research 直接挂钩。录音用 This fits with other recent research\nthat suggests taking short breaks is associated with higher levels of daily job\nperformance，fits with other recent research 与题干 agree with other recent\nresearch 直接呼应，that 从句即两方一致之处。\n【替换】taking short breaks is associated with higher levels of daily job performance ↔\nTaking mini-breaks means better job performance\nshort breaks＝mini-breaks，associated with＝means，higher＝better\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音虽说 cyberloafing is always good 也非绝对，但整体结论是摸鱼适度有益、能提效；\nB 说弊大于利，与讲者立场相反，更非两研究的吻合点。\n借用原文：we believe a bit of online shopping or surfing the internet at work could make workers\nmore productive\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 reduce worker turnover，但说的是减压可降低离职，并未说离职与允许摸鱼的\n时间长短相关联；C 拼出一个原文没有的因果，且不是与他研究的吻合点。\n借用原文：it may not only reduce worker turnover\nD) 与原文相反\n原文说常上网的受虐员工 less likely to want to quit，D 却说爱上网的最想辞职，方向正好\n反；且离职话题也不是与他研究的吻合点。\n借用原文：were less likely to want to quit than similar participants who didn't cyberloaf as much", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about Nebraska City in 1854 when Julius Morton moved there?", "options": ["A) There were no wooden buildings.", "B) There were environmental problems.", "C) There were no trees.", "D) There were few settlers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） There were no trees.\n【定位】When Julius Morton moved to Nebraska City in 1854, he faced a problem shared by many\nsettlers in the territory: It was a treeless plain. That meant no trees for building materials, or\nfor fuel.\n当朱利叶斯·莫顿于1854年迁居内布拉斯加城时，他面临着该地区许多移民共有的一个问\n题：这是一片没有树的平原。这意味着没有树木可作建筑材料，也没有树木可作燃料。\n【信号】时间词后的状况交代。录音开头复述了题干同样的时间地点 When Julius Morton moved\nto Nebraska City in 1854，紧跟着的 It was a treeless plain 就是对当地状况的直接交\n代，这种\"时间地点重现+随即说明情况\"是答案句的信号。\n【替换】It was a treeless plain ↔ There were no trees.\ntreeless = no trees\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 no trees for building materials（没有树作建筑材料），A 把它偷换成\nThere were no wooden buildings.（没有木建筑）。原文说的是缺\"建材来源\"而非\"已无木\n建筑\"，听到 building 这个原词就选会上当。\n借用原文：no trees for building materials\nB) 过度推断\n录音只说当地是没树的平原、缺建材和燃料，并未泛泛地说 There were environmental\nproblems.。B 把\"无树\"这一具体事实拔高成\"环境问题\"这种概括大词，属过度推断。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 a problem shared by many settlers（许多移民共有的问题），D 盗用 settlers\n这个原词编出 There were few settlers.（移民很少）。原文是说移民\"多\"且共有难题，并\n未说移民少，方向正相反。\n借用原文：a problem shared by many settlers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What did Julius Morton do that increased his influence in Nebraska City?", "options": ["A) He served as chairman of the Nebraska State Board of Agriculture.", "B) He urged the state to start the Nebraska State Gardening Society.", "C) He engaged himself in a large number of aesthetic projects.", "D) He founded a newspaper and used it to promote his ideas."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He founded a newspaper and used it to promote his ideas.\n【定位】As the forest grew, so did Morton's influence in Nebraska. This was largely because Morton\nfounded the Nebraska City News, the state's first newspaper, in which he frequently wrote\neditorials about the practical and aesthetic benefits of tree planting.\n随着森林的成长，莫顿在内布拉斯加的影响力也随之增长。这在很大程度上是因为莫顿创办\n了内布拉斯加城新闻报——该州第一份报纸，他常在报上撰写社论，论述植树在实用与审美\n两方面的益处。\n【信号】because 引出的因果落点。录音先说影响力增长，紧接 This was largely because Morton\nfounded the Nebraska City News，因果连词 because 直接把\"影响力增大\"的原因锁定\n到\"创办报纸\"这件事上，因果信号即答案信号。\n【替换】Morton founded the Nebraska City News, the state's first newspaper ↔ He founded a newspaper\n专名概括成普通名词\nhe frequently wrote editorials about the practical and aesthetic benefits of tree planting ↔\nused it to promote his ideas\n撰文宣传=推广理念\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说他 served on the Nebraska State Board of Agriculture（在董事会任职），A 改成\nserved as chairman of the Nebraska State Board of Agriculture（任董事会主席）。原\n文只说\"任职\"未说\"任主席\"，且这件事并非影响力增大的原因，既偷换身份又答非所问。\n借用原文：served on the Nebraska State Board of Agriculture\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说他 organized the Nebraska State Gardening Society（组织了园艺学会），B 盗\n用该机构名却把动作换成 urged the state to start the Nebraska State Gardening\nSociety（敦促州里去创办）。原文是他亲自组织、并非\"敦促州政府创办\"，且此事也非影响\n力之源，答非所问。\n借用原文：organized the Nebraska State Gardening Society\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 aesthetic 只是修饰社论内容（讲植树的实用与审美益处），C 把它拼成 engaged\nhimself in a large number of aesthetic projects（投身大量审美项目）。原文根本没说他\n搞了一堆审美项目，属盗用原词后无中生有。\n借用原文：the practical and aesthetic benefits of tree planting", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker say happened on April 10, 1872?", "options": ["A) A special prize was awarded to Julius Morton.", "B) One million trees were planted throughout Nebraska.", "C) The state government declared it the official Arbor Day.", "D) Nebraska earned the nickname \"the Tree Planters State\"."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） One million trees were planted throughout Nebraska.\n【定位】And on April 10, 1872, the first unofficial Arbor Day was celebrated throughout Nebraska.\nPrizes were awarded to counties, cities, and individuals who planted the largest number of\ntrees. That day, an astounding one million trees were planted in Nebraska—an average of\nmore than six for every man, woman and child in the state.\n于是在1872年4月10日，第一个非官方的植树节在内布拉斯加全境举行。奖项颁给了种树最\n多的县、市和个人。那一天，内布拉斯加种下了惊人的一百万棵树——平均每个男女老少每\n人种了六棵多。\n【信号】日期后\"That day\"复指。录音念出题干同一日期 on April 10, 1872，随后用 That day, an\nastounding one million trees were planted 复指该日并给出当天的核心数字，这种\"日期\n重现+That day 复指\"是答案句信号，把当天发生的关键事件标了出来。\n【替换】an astounding one million trees were planted in Nebraska ↔\nOne million trees were planted throughout Nebraska.\n去掉修饰即等值\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音那天确有颁奖 Prizes were awarded to counties, cities, and individuals，但发给的\n是种树最多的县市和个人，A 却说 A special prize was awarded to Julius Morton.（颁给\n莫顿一个特别奖）。原文没说奖给莫顿，盗用 prize 原词后偷换了获奖对象。\n借用原文：Prizes were awarded to counties, cities, and individuals\nC) 与原文相反\n录音明说那天是 the first unofficial Arbor Day（第一个非官方植树节），C 却说州政府\ndeclared it the official Arbor Day（宣布为官方植树节）。原文在 official 前有否定前缀\nun-，C 恰好把极性反了，与原文相反。\n借用原文：the first unofficial Arbor Day\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音确实念到 earning it the nickname \"the Tree Planters State\"，但那是 Since 1885,\nNebraska has planted more than 700,000 acres 之后才赢得的，并非 1872 年4月10日\n当天的事。D 盗用醒目专名却安错了时间，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Since 1885, Nebraska has planted more than 700,000 acres of trees, earning it the\nnickname \"the Tree Planters State\"", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn about modern humans according to the hypothesis commonly accepted in the 1990s?", "options": ["A) They spread across Europe and Asia in a few decades.", "B) They lived mostly in Africa for about 200,000 years.", "C) They preferred to live in Europe rather than in Asia.", "D) They moved out of Africa about 60,000 years ago."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They moved out of Africa about 60,000 years ago.\n【定位】Modern humans arose in Africa about 200,000 years ago. They then spread across Europe and\nAsia sometime after 60,000 years ago. This is the \"Out of Africa\" model, as it's commonly\nknown. In the 1990s, the hypothesis found widespread acceptance.\n现代人大约在二十万年前起源于非洲。随后在距今约六万年后的某个时期扩散到欧洲和亚\n洲。这就是众所周知的『走出非洲』模型。上世纪九十年代，这一假说获得了广泛接受。\n【信号】时间状语锁定旧假说内容。题干限定 in the 1990s 的旧假说，录音里 In the 1990s, the\nhypothesis found widespread acceptance. 把前文 spread across Europe and Asia\nsometime after 60,000 years ago 这段表述归到了那个旧假说名下，因此前面讲迁徙时间\n的那几句就是答案句。\n【替换】spread across Europe and Asia sometime after 60,000 years ago ↔\nThey moved out of Africa about 60,000 years ago.\n扩散到欧亚=走出非洲\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 They spread across Europe and Asia in a few decades. 直接搬用录音原词 spread\nacross Europe and Asia，听到就想选；但 in a few decades（几十年内）是录音根本没说\n的速度，讲者只说在六万年后扩散、并未说扩散只花几十年。\n借用原文：spread across Europe and Asia sometime after 60,000 years ago\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 They lived mostly in Africa for about 200,000 years. 偷换了 arose in Africa about\n200,000 years ago 里的 200,000：录音说的是『二十万年前起源于非洲』这个时间点，不\n是『在非洲住了二十万年』这个时长，听到 200,000 + Africa 就选会落坑。\n借用原文：Modern humans arose in Africa about 200,000 years ago.\nC) 未提及\n选项 They preferred to live in Europe rather than in Asia. 借用了原词 Europe 和 Asia，\n但录音只把欧洲与亚洲并列为扩散去向（Europe and Asia），从未比较二者、更没说『偏\n好欧洲胜过亚洲』，属凭空添加的偏好。\n借用原文：spread across Europe and Asia sometime after 60,000 years ago", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What has called into question the \"Out of Africa\" model?", "options": ["A) The discovery of two modern human teeth in China.", "B) The traces of human migration out of Africa to Asia.", "C) The human fossils discovered most recently in Africa.", "D) The Luna cave in Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The discovery of two modern human teeth in China.\n【定位】Take the recent discovery of two modern human teeth found in the Luna cave in China's\nGuangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region. When my research team and I dated these teeth, we\nfound they were more than 70,000 years old—a situation clearly impossible if modern\nhumans moved out of Africa only 60,000 years ago.\n以最近在中国广西壮族自治区娄洞发现的两颗现代人牙齿为例。当我和我的研究团队给这些\n牙齿测年时，我们发现它们已有七万多年的历史——如果现代人直到六万年前才走出非洲，\n这种情况显然是不可能出现的。\n【信号】Take…例子+测年结果证伪旧说。讲者用 Take the recent discovery of two modern\nhuman teeth 引出一个具体例子，并以 more than 70,000 years old 这个测年结果说明它\n与旧模型矛盾，正是这个『两颗牙齿』的发现 called into question 了该模型，故定位到这\n例子。\n【替换】the recent discovery of two modern human teeth found in the Luna cave in China ↔\nThe discovery of two modern human teeth in China.\n近乎原句的浓缩\n【排除】B) 答非所问\n选项 The traces of human migration out of Africa to Asia. 用的是全篇大主题词\nmigration out of Africa，听起来很对题；但题目问的是『具体什么动摇了模型』，动摇模\n型的是那个测年超七万年的牙齿发现，而非泛泛的『迁徙痕迹』，B 笼统得答非所问。\n借用原文：the idea that modern humans migrated out of Africa only 60,000 years ago\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 The human fossils discovered most recently in Africa. 借了录音的 recent /\ndiscovery 和 fossils 等原词，但把地点改成了 in Africa；动摇模型的发现明明在中国\n（Luna cave in China），不在非洲，听到 recent + discovered 就选会被骗。\n借用原文：many new human fossils found, particularly in China\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 The Luna cave in Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region. 把录音里原样念出的专有\n地名整段照搬，最易『听到原词就想选』；但动摇模型的是『在该洞发现的牙齿超七万年』\n这一发现，洞穴本身只是地点，并非动摇模型的证据，D 偷换了概念。\n借用原文：found in the Luna cave in China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the speaker want to show with the example of some monkeys in Africa?", "options": ["A) There must have been some reason for human migration.", "B) There have been changes in animals' living conditions.", "C) Humans adapted themselves to the environment there.", "D) Humans had access to abundant food sources there."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） There must have been some reason for human migration.\n【定位】The first question we should ask is why did modern humans leave Africa to begin with? If a\npopulation is perfectly adapted to a particular environment and has access to an abundance of\nresources, then there really is no reason to move or change. For instance, some monkeys in\nAfrica have a set of teeth that has hardly changed over the course of millions of years,\nindicating that they found a place that has worked for them.\n我们首先该问的是：现代人当初究竟为什么要离开非洲？如果一个种群完美适应了某个特定\n环境、又能获得充足的资源，那就真的没有理由迁移或改变了。比如，非洲的一些猴子，其\n牙齿在数百万年里几乎没有变化，这表明它们找到了一处适合自己的栖身之地。\n【信号】例子前的论点句+indicating回扣。猴子例子由 For instance 引出，它服务的论点写在前一\n句 then there really is no reason to move or change，又被例子末尾 indicating that\nthey found a place that has worked for them 回扣——合起来说明：环境好就没理由\n迁，反过来人类迁徙必有原因，故答案句锁定这组论点而非猴子牙齿细节。\n【替换】there really is no reason to move or change ↔\nThere must have been some reason for human migration.\n正话反说的等值推论\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项 There have been changes in animals' living conditions. 蹭了 animals（猴子是动\n物）这一关联，但录音说猴子牙齿 hardly changed、环境一直 worked for them，恰恰是\n『没变化』，B 把『没变』说成『有变』，与原文相反。\n借用原文：some monkeys in Africa have a set of teeth that has hardly changed\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Humans adapted themselves to the environment there. 借了原文 adapted、\nenvironment 等原词，但 perfectly adapted 在录音里说的是『假设种群（以猴子为例）\n适应了就不会迁』，是用来反衬人类要迁的前提，并非讲者想证明『人类适应了那里』，C\n张冠李戴又答非所问。\n借用原文：If a population is perfectly adapted to a particular environment\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Humans had access to abundant food sources there. 盗用原文 access to an\nabundance of resources 的近义词，但原文说的是『若有充足资源就没理由迁』这一假设\n前提，讲者举猴子恰是要说明人类迁了说明并非如此，D 把反衬前提错当成结论，且把\nresources 窄化成 food sources。\n借用原文：has access to an abundance of resources, then there really is no reason to move", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What question does the speaker raise close to the end of the talk?", "options": ["A) How humans settled down on the Arabian Peninsula.", "B) When modern humans started to disperse out of Africa.", "C) What path modern humans took to migrate out of Africa.", "D) Why fresh water is so important for human survival."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） What path modern humans took to migrate out of Africa.\n【定位】This brings us to another question: by which route did modern humans move out of Africa?\n这就把我们引向另一个问题：现代人究竟是沿哪条路线走出非洲的？\n【信号】another question引出的自问句。结尾段用 This brings us to another question 明确抛\n出讲者临近结尾的那个问题，冒号后的 by which route did modern humans move out\nof Africa 就是该问题本体，by which route 即问『路线』，故答案锁定此句。\n【替换】by which route did modern humans move out of Africa ↔\nWhat path modern humans took to migrate out of Africa.\nby which route=what path\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 How humans settled down on the Arabian Peninsula. 借了结尾出现的地名\nArabian Peninsula，听到就想选；但讲者只是把阿拉伯半岛作为可能路线的推测，并没有\n把『如何在阿拉伯半岛定居』当作他提出的那个问题，A 答非所问。\n借用原文：the earlier dispersals out of Africa and into Europe and Asia might have been across the\nArabian Peninsula\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 When modern humans started to disperse out of Africa. 用了 disperse out of\nAfrica 等原词，问的是『何时』；但『何时走出非洲』是前文（第二段牙齿测年）已讨论的\n旧问题，结尾真正抛出的是 by which route（哪条路线），B 把疑问词从 route 偷换成\nwhen。\n借用原文：by which route did modern humans move out of Africa\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Why fresh water is so important for human survival. 盗用结尾出现的 fresh water\n原词；但讲者提 Humans need daily access to reliable fresh water 只是引出路线问题的\n铺垫，并未把『为何淡水重要』作为他提出的问题，D 把铺垫句错当成所提问题。\n借用原文：Humans need daily access to reliable fresh water", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) indulging\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 after 后必跟动名词\n【依据】indulge in=纵情大吃，与“吃完一顿大餐”语义一致\n句意是“即使在 indulging in a huge meal（沉溺/纵享一顿大餐）之后还想继续吃”，\nindulge in 是固定搭配，且语义恰是“尽情享用一顿大餐”，与开篇 Imagine sitting down\nto a big dinner: a massive steak, a large portion of fried potatoes, and cake for\ndessert. 的“暴饮暴食”场景严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】B) comprised 搭配是 be comprised of，不接 in\ncomprised 的固定搭配是 be comprised of（由……组成），不与 in 连用，且这里没有 be 动\n词；语义“一顿大餐由……组成”也与“吃完后还想吃”的语境无关。\nE) diminishing 义为“递减”，与“暴饮暴食”反向\ndiminish in 表示“在……方面减少”，可这里要表达的是“纵享了一顿大餐”，diminishing in a\nhuge meal（在一顿大餐中递减？）语义讲不通，方向恰好相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 after 后必跟动名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) innumerable\n形容词\n【词性槽】correlates with ___ health issues，名词前作定语\n【依据】“无数的”健康问题，与肥胖危害的客观表述匹配\n原文是说肥胖 correlates with __(27)__ health issues——医学常识告诉我们肥胖会引发“多\n到数不清”的健康问题（心血管、糖尿病、关节、肿瘤……），innumerable 是常见且中性的\n描述。\n【竞争词】O) unmatchable 感情色彩偏褒，且语义不通\nunmatchable 表“无与伦比”，是积极/褒义评价词，用来形容“健康问题”从感情色彩到搭配都明\n显错位——说“无与伦比的健康问题”更像是把“好”和“坏”拼接起来，逻辑不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·correlates with ___"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) mortality\n名词\n【词性槽】increased ___ risk，名词前作定语\n【依据】mortality risk=死亡风险，肥胖危害的高频搭配\nis even linked to increased __(28)__ risk. 上承“肥胖关联无数健康问题”，再用 even 递\n进，意指“甚至与死亡风险升高相关”——mortality risk 是医学英语里极常见的固定搭配，\n且 even 的递进语气也只有“死亡”这级别的代价能配得上。\n【竞争词】A) attributes attribute risk 搭配不存在\nattributes 是“属性”，与 risk 不能复合构成“属性风险”这种意义，且语义也不递进。\nD) conservation 话题错位\nconservation 是“资源保护”，conservation risk 偏环保领域，与肥胖语境完全不接轨。\nL) plights 复数名词无法作单数前置定语，且无此搭配\nplights 用复数，且 plights risk 不是固定搭配，无法表达“困境风险”这种意思。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·increased ___ risk，名"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) attributes\n名词\n【词性槽】two main ___ + 冒号后两项并列，需复数名词\n【依据】attributes=特征，正好对应冒号后并列的两件特征\nIt is characterized by two main attributes：两个主要特征——一是“吃不饱”，二是“想久\n坐不动”，正好两条；attributes 在描写“现象/事物的标志性特征”时最常用，与\ncharacterized by 形成 collocation 闭环。\n【竞争词】D) conservation 单数不可数，不能跟在 two main 后，且话题不对\nconservation 一般不可数，前面带 two main 直接不合语法；语义“两个保护”也与“吃不饱+久\n坐”毫无关联。\nL) plights 感情色彩太重，且 characterized by plights 不自然\nplights 强调“悲惨困境”，是悲剧色彩词；这里只是中性描述现象的两个特征，用“两大困境”过于\n戏剧化，且 be characterized by two main plights 不是地道搭配。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— plights 像“健康困境”，但搭配与色彩都不对。在 It is\ncharacterized by two main __(29)__ 处，plights（困境）看似贴“insatiable hunger 是种\n烦恼”，但 characterized by 后用 attributes/features 等中性词才地道；选 plights 既不搭\n配，也戏剧化。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·two main ___ + 冒号后两项"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) odds\n名词\n【词性槽】at__with 是介词框架，中间只能是名词\n【依据】at odds with=与……相矛盾\n段首正在做对比——“永不满足的饥饿”这件事 This is at __(30)__ with how humans are\nbiologically programmed. 与人类生物程序“相矛盾”，at odds with 是这一意思的固定搭\n配，且段后描述古人“一吃多就想动”，正与“久坐不动”相反，逻辑闭环。\n【竞争词】A) attributes 无 at attributes with 搭配\nattributes 作名词指“特征、属性”，没有 at attributes with 的固定结构，硬塞语义也不通。\nD) conservation 话题不对\nconservation 指“资源保护”，与人体饮食机制毫无关联，且无 at conservation with 搭配。\nJ) mortality 另有归宿，且无此搭配\nmortality 是“死亡率”，要留给 28 空的 increased __ risk；且无 at mortality with 搭配。\nL) plights 无搭配，语义也不对\nplight 是“困境”，没有 at plights with；说“这与生物程序处于困境”也不通顺。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·at__with 是介词框架，中间只能是"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) regulatory\n形容词\n【词性槽】the brain's ___ mechanism，名词前作定语\n【依据】“调节性的”机制，与“防止体重过量积累”一一对应\ncompensation 被定义为 the brain's __(31)__ mechanism for preventing the\naccumulation of excess weight.——“防止过量体重堆积”正是一种“调节、调控”的功能，\nregulatory mechanism（调节机制）是生理学常见搭配，语义贴合。\n【竞争词】I) innumerable “无数的”修饰不可数的 mechanism 不通\ninnumerable 强调数量上无穷多，通常修饰复数可数名词；说“大脑无数的机制”既不贴合 a\nmechanism 的单数定指，也无法解释它“防止体重堆积”的具体功能。\nO) unmatchable 感情色彩偏褒，且不是科学常用搭配\nunmatchable 意为“无与伦比”，是评价色彩词；下文是中性、客观的生理机制描述，且\nunmatchable mechanism 几乎不出现于科学语篇。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the brain's ___ mech"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) inclined\n形容词\n【词性槽】naturally ___ to do，be ___ to do 表语结构\n【依据】be inclined to do=倾向做某事，唯一能接 to do\nbe inclined to do 是固定搭配，意为“倾向于……”，正好对应“早上吃多了，剩下时间自然就\n少吃”的补偿逻辑：if you eat one large meal in the morning, you are naturally __(32)__\nto eat less for the rest of the day.\n【竞争词】I) innumerable 不接 to do\ninnumerable 是“数不清的”，不与不定式连用，语法上直接不通；且语义“你是数不清的”更是\n荒谬。\nO) unmatchable 不接 to do，语义也不对\nunmatchable 强调“无与伦比”，既无 be unmatchable to do 的常规结构，也与“倾向少吃”的中\n性补偿描述毫无关系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·naturally ___ to do，"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) still\n副词\n【词性槽】worse ___ , 修饰比较级\n【依据】worse still=更糟的是，固定插入语\n六级里 worse still 是常用插入语，等同于 even worse / what's worse，专门承接更严重的\n事实——本句正是承接前文“补偿机制失灵”再升级，说“更糟的是相当多人吃到一半饥饿减\n弱、吃完反弹”，worse __(33)__, a significant number of them report __(34)__ hunger\nhalfway through an eating session 语义一致。\n【竞争词】F) far far 修饰比较级须前置\nfar 强化比较级时位置是 far worse（远远更糟），不能写成 worse far；且 far 作插入语没有\nworse far 这种习惯用法，结构不通。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— far 与 still 都能强化比较级，位置才是关键。看到 worse\n__(33)__ 极易选 far（far worse 在阅读里太常见），但本题空在 worse 之后，far 不能后置\n——记住 worse still 是固定插入语，far 必须前置。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·worse ___ , 修饰比较级"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) diminishing\n动词\n【词性槽】report ___ hunger，report 后接动名词或宾语\n【依据】“饥饿感正在减弱”，与 halfway through 时点契合\n句意是“相当多人报告吃到一半时 __ 饥饿感”——结合“饥饿感本应随进食而下降”的生理常\n识，diminishing hunger（正在减弱的饥饿感）正合理；后文 but, by the end of the\nmeal, they feel the same or higher levels of hunger than when they sat down. 用 but\n转折，对照出“吃到一半饥饿在减弱，吃完反而又回来或更甚”，逻辑闭环。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·report ___ hunger，re"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) attributes", "B) comprised", "C) conceded", "D) conservation", "E) diminishing", "F) far", "G) inclined", "H) indulging", "I) innumerable", "J) mortality", "K) odds", "L) plights", "M) regulatory", "N) still", "O) unmatchable"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) comprised\n动词\n【词性槽】be ___ of 被动/分词结构\n【依据】be comprised of=由……组成\nthe modern diet, which is __(35)__ of food high in sugar, carbohydrates and calories.\n句意为“现代饮食由高糖、高碳水、高热量的食物组成”，be comprised of 是该意的固定搭\n配，与全文“现代饮食是元凶”的结论严丝合缝。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be ___ of 被动/分词结构"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Socially prescribed perfectionism is described as one's self-esteem depending on other people's opinion.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Socially prescribed 三个字加自尊取决于他人看法\n【定位】Curran describes socially prescribed perfectionism as \"My self-esteem is contingent on what\nother people think.\"\nCurran 把社会规定型完美主义描述为：我的自尊取决于别人怎么想。\n【改写】Socially prescribed perfectionism ↔ socially prescribed perfectionism\n术语原样复现\none's self-esteem depending on other people's opinion ↔\nMy self-esteem is contingent on what other people think\ndepending on 等于 is contingent on，other people's opinion 等于 what other people think\n【辨邻】 C socially prescribed, or a desire to live up to others' expectations 给的是另一个角\n度的简短解释，且整段重心是 three types of perfectionism 的清单与上升百分比，\n不是题干要求的 self-esteem 定义。\nsocially prescribed, or a desire to live up to others' expectations", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Jessica Pryor has learned that some graduate students work such long hours in the lab that they have little time for entertainment or socializing.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Jessica Pryor 加 12 到 18 小时实验室\n【定位】She has heard of graduate students spending 12 to 18 hours at a time in the lab.\n她听说有研究生一次性在实验室待 12 到 18 个小时。\n【改写】Jessica Pryor has learned that some graduate students work such long hours in the lab ↔\nShe has heard of graduate students spending 12 to 18 hours at a time in the lab\nhas heard of 等于 has learned，spending 12 to 18 hours 上位为 such long hours\nthey have little time for entertainment or socializing ↔\npeople stop inviting them to social gatherings or dinner parties\n把社交场景概括为 entertainment or socializing\n【辨邻】 B Pryor, who is now with the Family Institute at Northwestern University, is trying\nto sound the alarm 是讲她的角色与态度，没有讲研究生在实验室待多少小时。\nAlong with other therapists, Pryor, who is now with the Family Institute at Northwestern\nUniversity, is trying to sound the alarm", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The author believes perfectionism may sometimes be constructive.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】the author believes 加 constructive\n【定位】Perfectionism can, of course, be a positive force.\n完美主义当然也可以是一种正向力量。\n【改写】perfectionism may sometimes be constructive ↔\nPerfectionism can, of course, be a positive force\nmay sometimes 等于 can of course，constructive 等于 a positive force\n【辨邻】 B Though it is often portrayed as a positive trait, Pryor and others say extreme\nperfectionism can lead to depression, anxiety, and even suicide——一个\nThough 让步从句之后立刻反转，全段立场是警告，而非赞成完美主义可以是建设\n性的。\nThough it is often portrayed as a positive trait, Pryor and others say extreme perfectionism can lead\nto depression, anxiety, and even suicide", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "It is found that perfectionism is getting more and more prevalent among college students.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】more and more prevalent among college students\n【定位】What's more, perfectionism seems to be on the rise. In a study of thousands of American,\nCanadian, and British college students published earlier this year, Thomas Curran of the\nUniversity of Bath and Andrew Hill of York St. John University found that today's college\nstudents report higher levels of perfectionism than college students did during the 1990s or\nearly 2000s.\n更进一步，完美主义似乎在上升。今年早些时候发表的一项针对美国、加拿大和英国数千名\n大学生的研究中，Curran 和 Hill 发现：今天的大学生报告的完美主义水平高于 1990 年代\n或 2000 年代初的大学生。\n【改写】perfectionism is getting more and more prevalent among college students ↔\nperfectionism seems to be on the rise\non the rise 等于 getting more and more prevalent，且 C 段全段以 college students 为研究\n对象\n【辨邻】 B a tendency among young adults and college students to strive for perfection 描\n述的是当下倾向，并没有比较过去与现在的水平。\na tendency among young adults and college students to strive for perfection in their work", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Some experts suggest parents and educators should prepare students for failures.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】parents and educators 加 prepare students for failures\n【定位】While educators and parents have successfully convinced students of the need to be high\nperforming and diligent, the experts told me, they haven't adequately prepared them for the\ninevitability of failure.\n尽管教育者和家长成功地让学生相信要做到高产高勤的必要性，但专家告诉我，他们并没有\n让学生为不可避免的失败做好充分准备。\n【改写】parents and educators should prepare students for failures ↔ educators and parents\n双主体原样复现，仅顺序对调\nshould prepare students for failures ↔\nthey haven't adequately prepared them for the inevitability of failure\n把段内的现状否定转写为题干的应然建议\n【辨邻】 I she encourages them to remember that one outcome is not a basis for a broad\nconclusion 主体是心理学家 Bach 对她的学生，跟 parents and educators 的应然建\n议不在同一层。\nWhen they don't get some award, she encourages them to remember that one outcome is not a basis\nfor a broad conclusion about the person's intelligence, qualifications, or potential for the future", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Some therapists warn that young adults tend to pursue perfection in their work.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】Some therapists warn 加 young adults\n【定位】Along with other therapists, Pryor, who is now with the Family Institute at Northwestern\nUniversity, is trying to sound the alarm about a tendency among young adults and college\nstudents to strive for perfection in their work—sometimes at any cost.\n和其他治疗师一起，Pryor 正在为一种倾向敲响警钟：年轻人和大学生都倾向于在工作中追\n求完美，有时候不惜任何代价。\n【改写】Some therapists warn ↔ Along with other therapists, Pryor\nSome therapists 等于 Pryor 与 other therapists 这一组主体\nwarn that young adults tend to pursue perfection in their work ↔\nis trying to sound the alarm about a tendency among young adults and college students to\nstrive for perfection in their work\nsound the alarm 等于 warn，strive for 等于 pursue\n【辨邻】 A Pryor told me 只是引述她讲的故事，画面里有研究生通宵实验室的细节，但没有警告\na tendency 这一动作。\nPryor told me\nC Thomas Curran of the University of Bath and Andrew Hill of York St. John\nUniversity found 的主体是研究人员的统计发现，不是治疗师发警告。\nThomas Curran of the University of Bath and Andrew Hill of York St. John University found that\ntoday's college students report higher levels of perfectionism", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Psychologist Amy Bach encourages her students to aim high but be content with something less than perfect.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Amy Bach 加 aim high but be content\n【定位】She tells them, \"Aim high, but get comfortable with good enough.\"\n她对学生们说：把目标定高，但要学会接受够好就行。\n【改写】Psychologist Amy Bach encourages her students ↔\nBach, who sees many students from Brown University\nBach 的身份与受众一致\nto aim high but be content with something less than perfect ↔\nAim high, but get comfortable with good enough\n金句原样 + good enough 等于 something less than perfect\n【辨邻】 K Brustein likes to get his perfectionist clients to create values that are important\nto them 主体是 Brustein，且策略不同。\nBrustein likes to get his perfectionist clients to create values that are important to them", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "A clinical psychologist finds perfectionism is widespread among his clients.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】A clinical psychologist 加 widespread among his clients\n【定位】Michael Brustein, a clinical psychologist in Manhattan, says when he first began practicing in\n2007, he was surprised by how prevalent perfectionism was among his clients, despite how\nlittle his graduate training had focused on the phenomenon.\n曼哈顿临床心理学家 Michael Brustein 说，他 2007 年刚开始执业时，对完美主义在客户\n中的普遍程度感到意外，而他研究生阶段的训练几乎没有涉及这种现象。\n【改写】A clinical psychologist ↔ Michael Brustein, a clinical psychologist in Manhattan\n身份标签原样命中\nperfectionism is widespread among his clients ↔\nhow prevalent perfectionism was among his clients\nwidespread 等于 prevalent，his clients 原样\n【辨邻】 G Brustein says his perfectionist clients tend to devalue their accomplishments 是\n描述客户行为模式，不是讲完美主义在客户中普遍出现这一发现。\nBrustein says his perfectionist clients tend to devalue their accomplishments\nB a tendency among young adults and college students to strive for perfection 的\n主体是 therapists 集体，并未点名 clinical psychologist 与他的 clients。\nAlong with other therapists, Pryor, who is now with the Family Institute at Northwestern\nUniversity, is trying to sound the alarm", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "In trying to overcome perfectionism, some people are still pursuing perfection.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】trying to overcome perfectionism 时仍在追求完美\n【定位】They try to be perfect, in other words, at no longer being perfect.\n换句话说，他们想在不再当完美主义者这件事上也做到完美。\n【改写】In trying to overcome perfectionism ↔ at no longer being perfect\n把不再完美 与 战胜完美主义 视作同义\nsome people are still pursuing perfection ↔ They try to be perfect\ntry to be perfect 等于 pursuing perfection\n【辨邻】 J hang the towels crooked or wear some clothing inside out 是治疗手段的具体例\n子，与题干悖论不在同一层。\nWe might have them hang the towels crooked or wear some clothing inside out", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "In pursuing perfection, some perfectionists fail to complete their tasks on time.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】fail to complete their tasks on time\n【定位】But others actually fall behind on work, unable to complete assignments unless they are,\nwell, perfect.\n但另一些人反而拖延工作，除非达到完美否则交不出作业。\n【改写】some perfectionists fail to complete their tasks on time ↔\nothers actually fall behind on work, unable to complete assignments unless they are, well,\nperfect\nfall behind 加 unable to complete 等于 fail to complete on time\n【辨邻】 F Meeting them becomes an all or nothing premise 描述心理层的恶性循环，没有\nfail to complete on time 这一具体行为。\nmake an archive of all their failures", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Which group of people does the author say has almost unrestricted access to children?", "options": ["A) Advertisers.", "B) Carers.", "C) Teachers.", "D) Doctors."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Which group of people does the author say has almost unrestricted access to\nchildren? 直接询问「哪一群人」，要求在原文里找出对应群体，是典型的事实细节追问。\n【拆句】主干为 Which group of people ... has almost unrestricted access to children，does\nthe author say 是插入语，去掉后剩「哪一群人对孩子有几乎不受限制的接触权」，结构平\n铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Yet despite all this, one group, which in no way has their best interests at heart, has almost\nunconstrained access.\n然而尽管如此，有一个群体，他们丝毫不把孩子的最大利益放在心上，却几乎不受限制地接\n触着孩子。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nadvertisers and marketers are allowed to condition even the youngest children ↔ Advertisers\n预判的「对孩子不友好却不受限制的群体」在 P2 被明示为广告商和营销者，与选项\nAdvertisers 直接对应。\nB) 与原文相反\nCarers 属于 every carer, teacher or doctor with whom they come into contact\n中被严格审查的一方，是「保护孩子」的角色，与「几乎不受限地接触孩子」的群体\n相反。\nC) 与原文相反\nTeachers 同样位于 every carer, teacher or doctor 列表中，是被审查的对象，不是\n「几乎不受限地接触孩子」的群体。\nD) 与原文相反\nDoctors 也是 every carer, teacher or doctor 列表中的被审查对象，原文未把医生列为\n「几乎不受限接触孩子」的群体。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What kind of people should we enable children to become according to the author?", "options": ["A) Those who look to fame and wealth for external and ultimate validation.", "B) Those who value themselves because of their contribution to society.", "C) Those who associate self-worth with the ability to handle money.", "D) Those who have developed a proper sense of their own identity."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What kind of people should we enable children to become according to the author?\n含 according to the author，要求复述原文已写明的内容，不要求跨段综合，仍是细\n节题。\n【拆句】主干为 What kind of people should we enable children to become，become 后省略\n表语补足语，由四个选项的 Those who... 补齐；结构清楚，唯一要注意的是 enable\nchildren to become＝「让孩子成长为」，主语是 we 不是 children。\n【定位】We're turning out little consumers rather than young citizens who will value themselves for\nwhat they contribute to the society in which they live.\n我们正在培养小消费者，而不是会因自己对所在社会的贡献而看重自身价值的年轻公民。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThose who look to fame and wealth for external and ultimate validation 对应 to\nlook to external things such as fame and wealth for validation，是作者批判而非倡\n导的形象。\nB) ✓ 正确\nyoung citizens who will value themselves for what they contribute to the society in which they\nlive\n↔ Those who value themselves because of their contribution to society\n选项 B 在主语「Those who」、动词「value themselves」、原因状语三处与定位句逐\n项对应，是作者正向倡导项的同义改写。\nC) 偷换概念\nThose who associate self-worth with the ability to handle money 把 learned to\nhandle money 这个被作者列出的「尚未具备」的前提，偷换成孩子应该成为的目标。\nD) 偷换概念\nThose who have developed a proper sense of their own identity 同样取自 Before\nchildren have even developed a proper sense of their own identity，仅是作者列出\n的孩子尚未具备的前提，并非最终倡导项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Many child development experts and educators call attention to research that suggests _____.", "options": ["A) Life-long consumerism is causing more and more cases of psychological problems.", "B) Increasing commercialization of education is eroding many children's self-esteem.", "C) The growing desire for wealth is contributing to a rising rate of depression.", "D) The craving for purchasing material things is nurtured throughout one's life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Many child development experts and educators call attention to research that\nsuggests _____. 是不完整陈述句，要求选项填入研究的内容，等同于追问该研究的结论事\n实，归为细节题。\n【拆句】主句 Many child development experts and educators call attention to research，that\nsuggests 后续宾语从句由选项补齐；call attention to＝呼吁关注，是熟词僻义提醒，主语\n动作就是「指出研究表明……」。\n【定位】Many psychologists, child development experts and educators point to research suggesting\nthat this emerging cradle-to-grave consumerism is contributing to growing rates of low self-\nesteem, depression and other forms of mental illness.\n许多心理学家、儿童发展专家和教育者援引研究指出：这种正在出现的「从摇篮到坟墓」的\n消费主义，正在导致低自尊、抑郁和其他形式心理疾病的发生率不断上升。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthis emerging cradle-to-grave consumerism ↔ life-long consumerism\ngrowing rates of low self-esteem, depression and other forms of mental illness ↔\nmore and more cases of psychological problems\nis contributing to ↔ is causing\n选项 A 的主语「终生消费主义」、谓语「正在导致」、宾语「越来越多心理问题」与定\n位句三项要点逐一同义对应，是最精确的复述。\nB) 无中生有\nincreasing commercialization of education 在原文中完全没有提及，作者批评的是面\n向孩子的广告与营销，不是教育自身的商业化。\nC) 以偏概全\nthe growing desire for wealth 只是原文 fame and wealth 的一半；a rising rate of\ndepression 也只是 low self-esteem, depression and other forms of mental illness\n的一项。两处都只取了原文的局部。\nD) 答非所问\nthe craving for purchasing material things is nurtured throughout one's life 说的\n是「物欲是如何被培养起来的」，而题干要求复述「研究表明的因果结论」——D 项把\n因果关系换成了「物欲是如何形成」，与题干追问方向不一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author imply about the impact of advertising?", "options": ["A) It is actually infectious to many rather than a few.", "B) It is rooted in our desire for material possessions.", "C) It is comparable to that of virus.", "D) It is literally limited to children."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】What does the author imply about the impact of advertising? 中的 imply 表明本题要\n求基于原文作推断，而非复述事实。\n【拆句】What does the author imply about the impact of advertising? 主干是 What ... the\nauthor imply，about the impact of advertising 限定推断对象；句子简洁，需注意\nimply 是「暗示」而非「明示」。\n【定位】It's also one that's forever developing new ways to persuade our children to desire material\npossessions, and because of advertisements' viral effect they only need to infect a few to\nreach the many.\n它（广告业）也是这样一个行业：不断开发新方法来诱使我们的孩子渴望物质；而由于广告\n的病毒式效应，他们只需感染少数即可触及多数。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthey only need to infect a few to reach the many ↔\nIt is actually infectious to many rather than a few\nthey only need to infect a few to reach the many ↔ rather than a few\n由 infect a few to reach the many 一步推出「广告其实波及多数而非少数」，正好是\nimply 题所要的「一步推断」型答案。\nB) 与原文相反\nrooted in our desire for material possessions 把因果反过来——原文是「广告诱使孩\n子产生物欲」（persuade our children to desire material possessions），不是「广\n告源于物欲」。\nC) 过度推断\ncomparable to that of virus 已经超出原文 viral effect 的比喻：原文只说「影响具\n有病毒式扩散效应」，并没断言「广告影响可与病毒影响相提并论」。两者程度差异\n明显。\nD) 与原文相反\nliterally limited to children 把广告影响范围缩限到「仅限于儿童」，而原文 infect a\nfew to reach the many 恰恰强调影响会扩散到「多数」，并非「仅限于儿童」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the opponents' argument against a complete ban on advertising to young children?", "options": ["A) It would deprive them of the chance to learn about products.", "B) It would render them unable to carve out unique identities.", "C) It would breach their freedom as would-be consumers.", "D) It would violate the rights endowed upon advertisers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the opponents' argument against a complete ban on advertising to young\nchildren? 直接问反对者给出的具体论据，对应原文中已写明的让步句，属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What is the opponents' argument，against a complete ban on advertising to\nyoung children 是后置修饰，整体含义是「反对者反对彻底禁广告时给出的理由」；注意\nagainst 修饰的是 argument，而非整个句子的否定。\n【定位】There'll be those who argue that would be a breach of freedom of speech and infringe the\nrights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products.\n会有人辩称（禁止广告）将构成对言论自由的侵犯，并损害企业「向幼儿洗脑、让他们索要\n产品」的权利。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\ndeprive them of the chance to learn about products 取自 Marketing may help\nsocialize children as consumers, inform them about products，是作者描述广告\n「益处」的内容，并非 P7 中的反方论据。\nB) 张冠李戴\nrender them unable to carve out unique identities 同样取自 help them carve out\nunique identities as they reach adulthood，是广告作为「益处」的功能描述，非 P7\n中反方对禁令的反对理由。\nC) 偷换概念\nbreach their freedom as would-be consumers 中 their 指 young children；但 a\nbreach of freedom of speech 中被侵犯的是「言论自由」，主语暗指「企业表达自\n由」，不是「孩子作为准消费者的自由」。\nD) ✓ 正确\ninfringe the rights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products ↔\nviolate the rights endowed upon advertisers\nthe rights of corporations to brainwash little children into demanding their products ↔\nthe rights endowed upon advertisers\n反方第二条论据「损害企业的权利」被选项 D 完整同义改写为「侵犯广告商被赋予的权\n利」，且两条论据中只有该条被选项明确复述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "Why are many people opposed to monitoring employee performance?", "options": ["A) It puts workers under constant pressure.", "B) It is universally deemed anti-human by nature.", "C) It does both mental and physical harm to employees monitored.", "D) It enables firms to squeeze maximal productivity from employees."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干Why are many people opposed to monitoring employee performance?直接索取\n一个被作者写在文中的客观原因,而非作者本人的推断或态度。\n【拆句】题干主干为被动语态are...opposed to,opposed to后接动名词短语monitoring employee\nperformance作介词宾语;句首Why仅起询问原因作用,无陷阱结构。\n【定位】Many oppose workplace surveillance, because of the inherent dehumanizing effect it has and\nthe relentless pressure it brings.\n许多人反对工作场所监控,因为它本身具有非人化的效果,并且带来持续不断的压力。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe relentless pressure it brings ↔ puts workers under constant pressure\n预判中\"持续压力\"一条被A项constant pressure逐词同义对应,A为定位句两项理由之一\n的精确改写,在四个选项中是唯一与原文有同义映射的项。\nB) 过度推断\n原文说监控具有inherent dehumanizing effect,只是描述其内在属性,并未声称这一点\n被普遍认同;B项把它升级为universally deemed anti-human,把\"内在属性\"偷换为\"举世\n公认\"。\nC) 无中生有\n原文只提及dehumanizing effect与relentless pressure,均偏心理与人格层面;C项添加\nphysical harm,原文无任何关于身体伤害的表述。\nD) 答非所问\nsqueeze maximal productivity复述的是企业立场——原文firms look to become\nmore efficient by squeezing more productivity from their workers是企业实施监控\n的动机,而非员工反对的理由,角色彻底错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the supporters' argument for workplace surveillance?", "options": ["A) It enables employees to refrain from dominating conversations.", "B) It enhances employees' identification with firms they work in.", "C) It can alert employees to intrusion into their privacy.", "D) It can protect employees against aggressive behavior."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干What is the supporters' argument直接索取支持方在文中明说的一项主张,不要求读\n者推断弦外之音。\n【拆句】题干为What引导的特殊疑问句,系动词is后接名词短语the supporters' argument for\nworkplace surveillance,for短语作定语限定argument的对象,结构平直无陷阱。\n【定位】While this may sound intrusive, exponents argue that it can also protect employees against\nbullying and sexual harassment.\n尽管这听起来具有侵扰性,支持者主张监控也可以保护员工免受欺凌和性骚扰。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文These can check how much time you spend talking, your volume and tone of\nvoice, even if you do not dominate conversations.是描述\"监控能检测谈话占比\"的功\n能,而非支持方提出的argument;A项enables employees to refrain from dominating\nconversations把\"检测是否dominate\"扭曲为\"使员工避免dominate\"。\nB) 无中生有\nB项enhances employees' identification with firms谈的是员工对公司的认同感,原文整\n段无任何提及\"员工认同公司\"的语义。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文While this may sound intrusive承认监控\"听起来侵犯隐私\",但支持者的argument\n是against bullying而非against intrusion;C项alert employees to intrusion into their\nprivacy反而把\"监控可警示员工隐私被侵犯\"作为主张,与支持方立场相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nprotect employees against bullying and sexual harassment ↔\nprotect employees against aggressive behavior\n预判中的\"保护员工免受bullying与sexual harassment\"被D项以aggressive behavior一\n词概括,protect...against结构原样保留,是定位句的标准同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author want to show by the example of different numbers of people interacting at lunch tables?", "options": ["A) Data analysis is key to the successful implementation of workplace surveillance.", "B) Analyzing data gathered from workers can yield something unexpected.", "C) More workmates sitting at a lunch table tend to facilitate interaction and idea sharing.", "D) It is hard to decide on how many people to sit at a lunch table without data analysis."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the author want to show by the example...中by the example明示题干在追\n问例子的论证目的而非例子的字面内容。\n【拆句】题干主干What...want to show中by the example为方式状语,后接of介词短语different\nnumbers of people interacting at lunch tables修饰example;considering动名词成分较\n长,但句意线性无歧义。\n【定位】Some of this data analysis can produce unexpected results. For example, it was found that\npeople who sat at 12-person lunch tables tended to interact, share ideas more and outperform\nthose who regularly sat at four-person tables, a fact that would probably have gone undetected\nwithout such data analysis.\n这种数据分析有时会产生出人意料的结果。例如,研究发现坐12人餐桌的人比坐4人餐桌的人\n更倾向于互动、分享想法,表现也更好,而这一事实如果没有这种数据分析很可能会被忽略。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文从未声称数据分析是监控成功实施的key;论点只是说\"数据分析能产生意外结果\",而\n非\"数据分析是监控成功的关键\";key一词无原文支撑。\nB) ✓ 正确\nSome of this data analysis can produce unexpected results. ↔\nAnalyzing data gathered from workers can yield something unexpected.\nB项主谓宾三段(Analyzing data/can yield/something unexpected)与原文(this data\nanalysis/can produce/unexpected results)逐成分对应,是论点句的标准同义重写。\nC) 答非所问\nC项More workmates sitting at a lunch table tend to facilitate interaction and idea\nsharing字面复述了例子内容,但题干问的是例子要说明的论点,不是例子本身,答非所问。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文末尾a fact that would probably have gone undetected without such data\nanalysis强调的是\"数据分析揭示了这一事实\",而非\"数据分析帮助决定餐桌人数\";D项把焦\n点偏到\"决定餐桌坐几人\"上。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does much of the positive effect of monitoring initiatives depend on?", "options": ["A) How frequently employees are to be monitored.", "B) What specific personal information is being excluded.", "C) What steps are taken to minimize their detrimental impact.", "D) How well bosses make known their purpose of monitoring."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does much of the positive effect of monitoring initiatives depend on?问的是文\n中明确写出的依赖条件,不需推断。\n【拆句】题干主干What does...depend on?中谓语动词是depend on,主语是较长的名词短语much\nof the positive effect of monitoring initiatives;of短语层层修饰,但语义线性,认出动词短语\ndepend on即可。\n【定位】A lot depends on how such monitoring initiatives are communicated and this could prevent\npossible revolts being staged.\n很多事情取决于这些监控举措是如何被传达的,这种沟通可能阻止潜在的抵制行动发生。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未提到监控的frequency(频率),只谈how communicated;A项把\"如何沟通\"偷换\n为\"多频繁地监控\",原文无任何对应。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文many such monitoring schemes use anonymised data谈匿名化处理,但语境\n是\"虽然匿名,员工仍恐惧\";B项specific personal information is being excluded把焦点\n偏到\"排除哪些信息\",且与题干\"positive effect depend on\"无对应。\nC) 过度推断\n原文确实提到detrimental一词,但句子是\"如果用监控减少休息,可能detrimental\";题干问\n的是\"positive effect depend on\",答案应回到A lot depends on的主句而非\ndetrimental前的条件句;C项把\"减少不利影响的步骤\"作为positive effect的依赖,属过度\n推断。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhow such monitoring initiatives are communicated ↔\nHow well bosses make known their purpose of monitoring\nD项核心动词make known精确对应原文communicated,主语bosses合理补出施动\n者,purpose of monitoring为补全语境的合理增添,语义聚焦于\"沟通\"这一关键变量。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What concern do monitoring initiatives cause among many staffers?", "options": ["A) They may empower employers excessively.", "B) They may erode the workplace environment.", "C) They may infringe upon staffers' entitled freedom.", "D) They may become counterproductive in the long run."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What concern do monitoring initiatives cause among many staffers?问的是员工在原\n文中明确表达的某项顾虑,不是作者推断。\n【拆句】题干主干What concern do monitoring initiatives cause中concern作cause的宾语前\n置,among many staffers为范围状语;倒装并未改变句意,主语是monitoring initiatives。\n【定位】Although many such monitoring schemes use anonymised data and participation is voluntary,\nmany staffers remain sceptical and fear an erosion of their civil liberties.\n尽管许多此类监控方案使用匿名数据且参与是自愿的,许多员工仍持怀疑态度,担心他们的公\n民自由被侵蚀。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文workplace surveillance could be empowering for staff中的empower指的是\"赋\n权员工\",而非\"赋权雇主\";A项empower employers excessively反转了方向,且\nexcessively无原文支撑。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文用an erosion of their civil liberties,erosion的对象是公民自由,而非workplace\nenvironment;B项保留了erosion的同根词erode,但把宾语偷换为\"工作环境\",熟词陷阱。\nC) ✓ 正确\nan erosion of their civil liberties ↔ infringe upon staffers' entitled freedom\n预判的\"公民自由被侵蚀\"被C项以infringe upon...entitled freedom精确改写,主谓宾结\n构对应,语义内核完整保留。\nD) 张冠李戴\nan unpopular tool of oppression that proves counterproductive是作者在末段的\n两面性总评,主体是作者而非many staffers;D项把作者的总评搬来作员工的顾虑,主体\n错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
